% As the ionizing field strength increases, orbital distortion is responsible in the change of 
% the relative magnitude of the rates at parallel orientations to those at antiparallel orientations as the field strength increases. The locations of maxima and minima in the orientation dependence is generally unaffected.}
% \myorange{Without orbital distortion, the shape of orientation-dependent ionization rate curves is independent of the field. If orbital distortion is taken into account, the relative magnitude between the rates at orientations parallel and antiparallel to the dipole vector will change with the field strength, while the locations of minima and maxima are generally unchanged.}
% \myorange{Orbital distortion typically modifies orientation-dependent ionization rates by modifying the relative magnitude of the rates at orientations with the field parallel to the dipole moment to those at antiparallel orientations without affecting the location of maxima or minima.} 
% \myorange{However, when higher parabolic channels are significant, their interplay with the first-order orbital distortion effects can change a minimum in orientation-dependent rates to a maximum.}


\pink{Due to the profound effects orbital distortion and higher parabolic channel contributions can have on ionization rates at high field strengths, it is prudent to use a method for calculating rates that can account for these effects. In the present work, we have  shown that OE-WFAT(1) is up to the task, especially after reformulation using partial-wave expansion to greatly enhance its efficiency. We found, for example, a speed-up of about $1.5\times10^4$ times in calculations on \molbrmet{} as compared to the explicit method with virtually no loss of accuracy. OE-WFAT(1) is most suitably used for molecules with low correlation effects so that the single-active electron approximation is valid. For correlated molecules, extending it to the many-electron treatment, resulting in ME-WFAT(1), is necessary.}

