Finally, we define constrained patterns and their instances.
\begin{definition}[constrained term~\cite{KN13frocos,Kop17,FKN17tocl}]
% Let $\Sigma' \subseteq \Sigma = \cD \cup \cC$.
A \emph{constrained term} over $\Sigma$ is a pair $\CTerm{t}{\psi}$ of a term $t:\iota$ and a constraint $\psi$.
%\Todo{such that $\Var(\psi) \subseteq \Var(t)$}.
The sort of $\CTerm{t}{\psi}$ is $\iota$.
%A constrained term $\CTerm{t}{\psi}$ is called a \emph{constrained $\Sigma'$-term} if $t$ is $\Sigma'$-term.
A constrained term $\CTerm{t}{\psi}$ is called \emph{linear} if $t$ is linear;
% A constrained term
$\CTerm{t}{\psi}$ is called 
\emph{linear w.r.t.\ logical variables} (LV-linear, for short) if $t$ is linear w.r.t.\ $\Var(\psi)$;
% A constrained term
$\CTerm{t}{\psi}$ is called 
\emph{value-free} if $t \in T(\Sigma\setminus \Val,\cV)$;
%A constrained term
$\CTerm{t}{\psi}$ is called 
a \emph{constrained pattern} if $t$ is a pattern.
\end{definition}
