\begin{convention}[Convention on Term Equivalence]
% In this paper, we consider a renamed variant of a term $t$ to be identical with $t$.
From the perspective that a (constrained) pattern represents the set of its ground instances, we consider a renamed variant $\CTerm{t'}{\psi'}$ of a constrained term $\CTerm{t}{\psi}$ to be identical with $\CTerm{t}{\psi}$, while we require $\psi'$ to be semantically % but not syntactically
equivalent to $\psi$.
To distinguish such equivalence from the usual one, we write $\CTerm{t}{\psi} \doteq \CTerm{t'}{\psi'}$:
$\CTerm{t}{\psi} \doteq \CTerm{t'}{\psi'}$ if and only if there exists a renaming $\delta$ such that $t = t'\delta$, $\Var(t)\cap\Var(\psi) = \Var(t'\delta) \cap \Var(\psi'\delta)$, and $((\exists \vec{x}.\ \psi) \Leftrightarrow (\exists \vec{y}.\ \psi'\delta))$ is valid, 
where
$\{\vec{x}\} = \Var(\psi) \setminus \Var(t)$
and
$\{\vec{y}\} = \Var(\psi'\delta) \setminus \Var(t'\delta)$.
The equivalence is also used for unconstrained terms:
$t \doteq t'$ if and only if $\CTerm{t}{\symb{true}} \doteq \CTerm{t'}{\symb{true}}$.
Given a set $P$ of constrained terms, 
