\section{Introduction}
Talking head generation is crucial for the metaverse and a wide range of applications, including immersive telepresence and augmented or virtual reality. This task involves generating a speaking portrait from a single image, guided by the corresponding audio and other auxiliary modalities. Lip synchronization for portrait generation has been extensively studied in computer vision. However, generating realistic and efficient talking head videos remains a persistent challenge.

The early methods~\cite{Pham_Cheung_Pavlovic_2017, prajwal2020lip} based on Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs)~\cite{Goodfellow_Pouget-Abadie_Mirza_Xu_Warde-Farley_Ozair_Courville_Bengio_Delhi} were the primary solution for audio-to-portrait translation, demonstrating relatively accurate lip synchronization. However, these methods remain limited in capturing diverse speaking styles, expressive emotions, and natural head movements. Recently, diffusion models~\cite{Dhariwal_Nichol_2021, Ho_Jain_Abbeel_Berkeley, Sohl-Dickstein_Weiss_Maheswaranathan_Ganguli_2015} have significantly advanced generative modeling, enabling the creation of highly diverse and high-fidelity images and videos. Motivated by this progress, significant research efforts~\cite{cui2024hallo2, chen2024echomimic, jiang2024loopy, shi2024joyhallo, Tian_Wang_Zhang_Bo, xu2024hallo, xu2024vasa, cao2024joyvasa, li2024ditto} have been made aimed at extending these innovations to the development of portrait image animation. 

These methods are typically categorized into single-stage and two-stage frameworks. The former~\cite{cui2024hallo2, chen2024echomimic, jiang2024loopy, shi2024joyhallo, Tian_Wang_Zhang_Bo, xu2024hallo} rely on a VAE-style latent space to directly generate video content from the audio input, integrating both motion and appearance in one pass. Despite being conceptually simple and trainable from end to end, two major limitations still persist, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:compare}: 1) inefficient inference process. Most diffusion-based approaches must account for not only the diverse mapping of lip movements but also the rendering of visual appearances. This dual consideration significantly increases computational costs, making these methods impractical for real-time applications. 2) Limited capability for long-term video generation and visual artifacts. Methods like EMO~\cite{Tian_Wang_Zhang_Bo} and Hallo~\cite{xu2024hallo} generate new video content by conditioning on previous frames. These prior frames provide motion information for subsequent generation and simultaneously leak appearance details, leading to the progressive accumulation of pixel errors and deterioration in overall video quality. 

The two-stage methods~\cite{xu2024vasa, cao2024joyvasa, li2024ditto} use diffusion models to generate low-dimensional intermediate motion representations, simplifying the learning process. However, the effectiveness of these methods is often compromised by inaccurate representations. This limitation mainly arises from the inherently one-to-many nature of the audio-to-motion task. The commonly used unidirectional cross-attention mechanism fails to support sufficient multi-modal interaction, leading to inconsistent motion sequences when conditioned on arbitrary identities and audio inputs, as discussed in Sec.~\ref{sec:4.3.1}. 
Motivated by recent lip-to-speech tasks~\cite{9956600, prajwal2020learning}, visual information can provide complementary contextual cues to audio. Sufficient multi-modal interaction helps disambiguate motion generation, resulting in more accurate and identity-consistent outputs.

In this paper, we propose a novel framework named MoDA, designed to generate realistic talking head videos from a single portrait image. MoDA first establishes a joint parameter space that bridges motion generation and neural rendering, consisting of a disentangled motion-appearance representation~\cite{guo2024liveportrait} along with the corresponding audio, emotion, and identity information. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:liuchengtu1}, MoDA introduces Multi-modal Diffusion Transformer (MMDiT)~\cite{esser2403scaling}, equipped with rectified flow~\cite{liu2022flow}, as the backbone diffusion framework to facilitate multi-modal interaction. In this design, MoDA can dynamically adapt audio features based on motion, identity, and emotion, thereby improving the accuracy of motion generation.

However, there are semantic overlaps between modalities within the joint parameter space: for example, audio inherently contains emotional and identity-related cues. Independently learned weights hinder the performance of the model. To facilitate effective feature fusion, a coarse-to-fine fusion architecture is further proposed to progressively enhance feature integration across modalities. Initially, the model maintains modality-specific weights to capture the unique characteristics of each condition. In the intermediate stage, audio and auxiliary conditions, such as identity and emotion, begin to share attention weights. Finally, all modalities are merged into a unified representation space, allowing holistic fusion and improving the overall generative expressiveness. Moreover, to further encourage motion diversity while maintaining temporal consistency, this paper provides an optional Adaptive Lip-motion Synchronization Expert (ALSE), which can be integrated during training.

The contributions of our method can be summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item This paper proposes MoDA, a novel multi-modal diffusion framework that generates high-fidelity talking head videos from a single portrait image, audio, and additional modalities. 
\item A coarse-to-fine fusion strategy is designed to progressively integrate noise motion with audio and other modalities, enabling effective cross-modal interaction.
\item Extensive evaluations on public datasets demonstrate that our method outperforms contemporary alternatives in visual quality and quantitative metrics.
\end{itemize}
