\subsubsection{Visualization Results in Complex Scenarios.}
We further investigate MoDA's generation performance in complex scenarios. Specifically, for the visual modality, we utilize portraits of both real humans and animated characters, each paired randomly with various audio types, including speech, singing, recitation, and others. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:shiyantu2}, our method demonstrates strong performance in various complex scenarios. In addition, it is capable of efficient long-duration inference and fine-grained facial expression control.
\subsection{Ablation Study}
To validate the effectiveness of our method components, we deactivate each of them and report result in Table~\ref{tab:comparison3}. For more ablation experiments and implementation details, please refer to the supplementary materials.
\subsubsection{Cross-attention}\label{sec:4.3.1}
We replace our model with a Cross-Attention-Based Architecture (CABA) to perform multi-modal fusion for evaluation purposes. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:comparison3}, this substitution results in a performance drop in all metrics. Moreover, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:ablation}, the subject exhibits mouth-closing failures when lacking deep cross-modal interaction. To investigate this, we conducted two experiments under the same settings used in the w/ CABA variant as shown Fig.~\ref{fig:ablation}:
1) replacing the audio with the same image did not resolve the mouth closure issue (Replace audio);
2) replacing the image with the same audio addressed the issue (Replace image).
These results suggest that cross-attention fails to adapt audio features to different identity conditions. In contrast, MoDA enables dynamic adaptation of audio features based on contextual factors such as motion, identity, and emotion.

\subsubsection{Coarse-to-Fined Feature Fusion and Interaction}
We replace the Coarse-to-Fine Feature Fusion with a fully four-stream architecture (w/o C2F). As shown in Table~\ref{tab:comparison3}, this design leads to performance degradation, particularly in synchronization and motion quality, emphasizing the necessity of progressive feature fusion for effective cross-modal integration. These results indicate that independently learning weights introduces substantial redundant parameters, resulting in convergence challenges (with 904M parameters in the w/o C2F variant). In contrast, by gradually sharing weights, the proposed coarse-to-fine strategy significantly reduces the parameter count to 370M, leading to both greater efficiency and improved performance.
\subsection{Discussion}
A key limitation of our framework lies in the performance constraints introduced by the first-stage model, including the appearance encoder, motion encoder, and decoder. In particular, the Liveportrait model used at this stage performs poorly when handling large pose variations, leading to a degradation in output quality. Moreover, it often produces unnatural distortions when processing subjects with complex head accessories. These issues may arise from limitations in the training data or intrinsic constraints of the GAN-based generator architecture.

To address these issues, future work will explore more robust first-stage generation techniques. We plan to improve the training dataset by incorporating a wider range of head pose variations and complex head accessories. In addition, we will investigate more advanced video generation models~\cite{yang2024hunyuan3d, kong2024hunyuanvideo, hu2022lora, liu2024sora} to further improve the quality and expressiveness of the result.
