\section{Introduction}
\subsection{Literature Review}
%\subsection{Background and Literature Review}
\IEEEPARstart{D}{istributed learning} (DL) mitigates computational bottlenecks in conventional centralized machine learning (ML) by leveraging edge device (client) capabilities. However, decentralization introduces significant communication overhead arising from the extensive transmission of datasets. To overcome the communication bottleneck in DL, federated learning (FL) enables clients to collaboratively train models on local datasets without sharing raw data, thus significantly reducing communication costs and inherently enhancing data privacy by keeping training data local \cite{lee2024federated, ren2025advances}. 

Despite the protection offered by prohibiting raw data sharing, FL systems remain vulnerable to indirect privacy leakage in training models. Even without direct access to original datasets, adversaries can still infer sensitive information through trained local models, making FL susceptible to various types of model attacks, such as inversion attacks (IA) and membership inference attacks (MIA). In IA, adversaries aim to reconstruct the original input data from the model parameters.  In MIA, adversaries attempt to infer whether a specific data patch was used in the training by analyzing the observed models. To defend against these and other attacks, a widely adopted strategy is to inject artificial noise into the local model updates to obscure sensitive information, e.g., the well-known Gaussian mechanism \cite{dwork2014algorithmic}. 
To evaluate the extent of information leakage of each client's contribution, local mutual information privacy (LMIP) and local differential privacy (LDP) are commonly applied as effective metrics that quantify the information disclosed by a given mechanism \cite{fang2024privacyleakagednnssurvey, 9448019,nguyen2023active}.
While a number of studies \cite{saha2024privacy,kulynych2024attack} claim the effectiveness of Gaussian mechanisms with provable convergence, their practical applicability remains constrained by the inherent utility-privacy trade-off. Strong privacy requires an injection of substantial noise, which can severely impair model accuracy and impede convergence. Conversely, ensuring stable convergence and acceptable training performance requires the added noise to remain sufficiently small. This tension creates a fundamental conflict between convergence and privacy in FL.

Fortunately, the issue discussed above can be effectively addressed by secure aggregation \cite{10.1145/3133956.3133982,elgabli2024novel}, which introduces noise with specially designed structures that cancel out during aggregation, thereby preserving privacy while maintaining convergence speed. This makes secure aggregation an attractive solution in privacy-sensitive FL applications, where both privacy protection and model utility are essential.
%Among the techniques developed to protect against a curious server, secure aggregation has proven impactful. 
%It enables the exact reconstruction of the global model while ensuring that the server cannot infer any individual information. 
However, achieving secure aggregation highly relies on reliable communication, as unpredictable link disruptions will break the carefully coordinated structure of secret keys, which are essential for accurate and exact aggregation at the server. 
Recent advances in dropout-resilient secure aggregation methods include pairwise additive masks based on Shamir's Secret Sharing (SSS) \cite{10.1145/3133956.3133982}, the integration of coded dataset sharing with SSS \cite{schlegel2023codedpaddedfl}, and the application of maximum distance separable (MDS) random variables in secret key constructions \cite{zhao2022information}.
%Recent advances in dropout-resilient secure aggregation have attempted to address this issue. Notable methods include the use of pairwise additive masks based on Shamir's Secret Sharing (SSS) \cite{10.1145/3133956.3133982}, the integration of coded dataset sharing with SSS \cite{schlegel2023codedpaddedfl}, and the application of maximum distance separable (MDS) random variables in constructing secret keys\cite{zhao2022information}.
%Recent advances in dropout-resilient secure aggregation include a pairwise additive mask built upon Shamir's secret sharing (SSS) \cite{10.1145/3133956.3133982}, integration of gradient coding (GC) and SSS \cite{schlegel2023codedpaddedfl}, construction of maximum distance separable (MDS) random variables \cite{zhao2022information}.
While these techniques offer perfect privacy by ensuring zero information leakage, they face several limitations when applied to practical FL scenarios. First, data sharing is inherently restricted in FL systems due to privacy and regulatory constraints, limiting the feasibility of schemes that rely on extensive coordination or data exchange \cite{10.1145/3133956.3133982, schlegel2023codedpaddedfl}. Second, the mapping between real-valued model parameters and the finite-field arithmetic used in many secure aggregation protocols remains unclear and non-trivial, raising concerns about the practical applicability and robustness of these methods in real-world deployments \cite{zhao2022information}.

Another challenge arising from unreliable communication is the strict sub-optimality of the federated optimization. Data generated at the network edge is inherently uncontrollable and typically follows non-independent and identically distributed (non-IID) patterns across clients. This heterogeneity causes local model updates to deviate significantly from the global objective, leading to \textit{client drift}\cite{karimireddy2019scaffold}. As a result, the global model may converge toward the optimum along unstable or misaligned directions. %impairing its convergence speed. 
This problem is further exacerbated by unreliable communication, which may result in statistically uneven local contributions, driving the global model toward a stationary point that can significantly deviate from the true optimum. This phenomenon, referred to as objective inconsistency \cite{wangTackling}, results in strict sub-optimality and makes the trained model untrustworthy. 
Existing approaches include heterogeneity-aware client sampling \cite{weng2025heterogeneity}, adaptive resource allocation\cite{wang2021quantized}, heterogeneity-aware aggregation \cite{zheng2023federated,perazzone2022communication}, collaborative relaying \cite{saha2022colrel,yemini2023robust}, etc.. But they all rely on precise prior information to ensure optimality, thus increasing the implementation complexity in practical deployments. In contrast, \cite{wang2022unified} eliminates this need by appropriate accumulation mechanisms. Further improvements are made to handle unknown client participation statistics\cite{wang2024lightweight} and to adapt to non-stationary participation patterns\cite{xiang2024efficient}. 
However, these methods are prone to divergence, as gradient accumulation after several consecutive transmission failures can grow excessively. 
Other approaches include retransmission \cite{zang2023general,10109680}, which may prolong the total training time, and reusing the latest observed updates as substitutes for the missing ones \cite{jhunjhunwala2022fedvarp}, which may introduce incorrect statistics and cause sub-optimality. 


Another line of work applies coding strategies for straggler mitigation, eliminating the need for prior knowledge. For example, \cite{10802992} applies the MDS code to reduce the outage frequency in FL, but a consistent objective is not guaranteed in heterogeneous networks. In contrast, gradient coding (GC) has emerged as an effective approach that guarantees the optimality of FL without requiring prior knowledge of network conditions. In particular, GC employs cyclic gradient codes to enable exact global model recovery despite arbitrary communication patterns \cite{tandon2017gradient}. Several extensions of GC have been proposed to address various practical constraints and scenarios \cite{krishnan2021sequential, ye2018communication, wang2019heterogeneity, 9609019}. However, these methods typically impose heavy computational burdens due to dataset replication across clients. To overcome this limitation, \cite{10901785} proposed cooperative GC (CoGC), a gradient-sharing framework to recover the exact sum, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:CoGC_framework}. The CoGC greatly improves communication-computation-efficiency and ensures convergence to the optimal solution over heterogeneous networks without requiring prior knowledge of network statistics.

