\subsection{Motivations}
%The CoGC still confronts unresolved privacy risks, despite its many advantages. The risks arise during the gradient-sharing phase, where clients honestly exchange their local model updates, potentially exposing sensitive information. On the other hand, t
The CoGC still confronts unresolved privacy risks, especially during the gradient-sharing phase. On the other hand, 
the binary nature of the GC decoding mechanism provides a promising solution to the problem of unreliable communication in secure aggregation, as it allows either the exact recovery of the global model or nothing meaningful at all in the presence of client dropouts. 
This property ensures that the structure of aggregation remains intact whenever the number of received updates reaches the threshold. Following this intuition, we arrive at the first fundamental research question explored in this article:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*, topsep=1pt, itemsep=1mm, parsep=0pt, label=$\blacktriangleright$] 
    \item \textit{Is it possible to keep exact recovery of the global model while preserving privacy globally, despite unreliable communication?} (\textbf{C1})
\end{itemize}
%If the added noises are carefully structured to cancel out during server aggregation, privacy can be preserved without compromising the global model performance. 
By incorporating the idea of secure aggregation into the CoGC framework, this challenge can be addressed. 
The answer lies in the careful design of the additive noise (secret keys). 
However, flexibility in secret key design introduces a new issue: inconsistent and uneven privacy guarantees between clients. This leads to the second key research question, concerning fairness in privacy preservation:  
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*, topsep=1pt, itemsep=1mm, parsep=0pt, label=$\blacktriangleright$] 
\item \textit{How can secret keys be designed to ensure fair privacy preservation across clients while still enabling secure aggregation?} (\textbf{C2})
\end{itemize}
This article approaches these questions systematically, formally defining and addressing them from the perspective of secret key constructions. 
The significance of our work is three-fold: 
\textit{(i)} We ultimately resolve the model privacy issues in CoGC at all levels, while preserving its advantages in computation and communication efficiency;
\textit{(ii)} Meanwhile, the proposed scheme offers an alternative secure aggregation approach that is resilient to random client dropout, without requiring dataset sharing or multiple rounds of communication with the server;
\textit{(iii)} We formally formulate and address the secure aggregation problem for FL in the real field, making our solution practical and directly applicable. Additionally, we introduce and formalize fairness as a real-field privacy metric, providing both clear motivation and a principled foundation.
%In this paper, we formally formulate and address the secure aggregation problem for FL in real-valued fields. 



%%%%%%%%%----------------------------------------------------
%\vspace{-0.5em}
\subsection{Our Contributions} 
All conclusions presented in this work are supported by rigorous theoretical analysis and extensive experimental validations. The key contributions are detailed below.
\vspace{0.5em}\\
\noindent(\textbf{C1}) To achieve both resilience and secure aggregation under unreliable communication, we propose a novel Secure CoGC scheme, termed SecCoGC. %, designed explicitly. 
The SecCoGC guarantees optimal convergence performance in heterogeneous federated networks, enables exact reconstruction of the global model at the server, and can provide arbitrarily strong user privacy globally. The construction conditions and implementation procedures are tailored to be directly compatible with most existing FL frameworks. Furthermore, the proposed construction ensures privacy protection against any decoding mechanisms, providing comprehensive security against various model attacks.
\vspace{0.5em}\\
\noindent(\textbf{C2}) To address fairness concerns in privacy preservation, we propose a Fair and Secure CoGC (Fair-SecCoGC) scheme that ensures equitable privacy protection for all participating clients, while preserving the full benefits of the original SecCoGC.
After showcasing the fairness issues by examples, we establish rigorous theoretical foundations and a general construction approach for fair secret keys.
Importantly, we present an extremely simplified yet effective construction for fair secret keys, that %significantly reduces computational complexity, 
almost does not need computation. 
To the best of our knowledge, this work is the first to formally identify user fairness as a privacy metric in FL.
\vspace{0.5em}\\
%\noindent(\textbf{C3}) We conduct a thorough evaluation of the proposed method's performance, including both learning performance and the privacy protection. Specifically, we assess the reliability of global model reconstruction at the server. Next, we analyze the non-convex convergence rate of the method under arbitrary choices of local solvers, making the analysis applicable to a wide range of real-world scenarios.  Lastly, we perform a comprehensive assessment of the privacy preservation provided by the proposed method from multiple perspectives. A detailed evaluation of LMIP and LMIDP during model transmission is conducted at each level in FL.  
\noindent(\textbf{C3}) To ensure a comprehensive evaluation of privacy preservation, we provide multi-view and in-depth theoretical insights for the model privacy in SecCoGC and Fair-SecCoGC, taking unreliable communication into account, and at all levels of the training process. These results, in turn, ensure that the key constructions are both theoretically sound and practically deployable. Moreover, integrating theoretical privacy measures into the actual learning process can offer valuable insights and inspiration for future research.
\vspace{0.5em}\\
\noindent(\textbf{C4}) To ensure a tractable analysis of the proposed methods, we evaluate the system reliability of SecCoGC and Fair-SecCoGC under arbitrary network conditions. Furthermore, we analyze their convergence behavior in non-convex settings. Our theoretical results yield a standard convergence rate of $\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{T}}\right)$ under arbitrary privacy levels and arbitrary local solvers. 
Notably, the convergence analysis also applies to CoGC and GC-based distributed training, while accommodating arbitrary local solvers for broader applicability.
 %evaluating LMIP and LMIDP during model transmission at each stage of the FL process. 

%%%%%%%%%----------------------------------------------------
%\vspace{-0.5em}
\subsection{Paper Organization} 
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section II introduces the system model and necessary preliminaries. Section III presents the formulation of secure aggregation in the real field along with the proposed secure CoGC scheme. Section IV extends this by incorporating fairness considerations into the secure CoGC design. Privacy guarantees at each stage in FL are evaluated in Section V and Section VI. In Section VII, we analyze the system reliability of the proposed method, while Section VIII examines its convergence behavior. The performance of the proposed method is demonstrated through numerical comparisons with benchmark approaches in Section IX. Finally, conclusions are drawn in Section X, and supporting proofs are provided in the appendices.


