\section{Introduction} 
The standard $\Lambda$ Cold Dark Matter ($\Lambda$CDM) model has been remarkably successful in explaining the universe's large-scale structure, from galaxy clustering to cosmic microwave background anisotropies. However, it faces persistent challenges on galactic and sub-galactic scales. These include the cusp-core problem, where $\Lambda$CDM simulations predict steep central density profiles in dark matter halos, conflicting with observations of flat cores in dwarf galaxies \citep{Flores1994, Moore1994, deBlok2010}; the missing satellites problem, which originally highlighted the discrepancy between the abundance of predicted subhalos and observed dwarf galaxies \citep{Klypin1999, Moore1999} but is now largely considered resolved (recent studies suggest that $\Lambda$CDM may predict a deficit of satellites rather than an excess; \citealt{Kim2018, Muller2024}), and the too-big-to-fail problem, where simulated massive subhalos are too dense to host observed bright satellites \citep{Boylan-Kolchin2011, Tollerud2014}. 

Since the success of CDM is exceptionally well established on linear scales, it follows that any alternative model of dark matter needs to match the behaviour of CDM on large scales in order to constitute a viable model of structure formation. One such alternative is fuzzy dark matter (FDM), which is composed of non-interacting ultralight bosons with masses $m_\mathrm{b} \sim 10^{-22} \, \mathrm{eV}$ \citep{Hu2000,Hui2017}. These particles exhibit coherent dynamics and a wave-like behaviour on galactic scales due to their large de Broglie wavelength, which naturally suppresses small-scale structure formation. This suppression offers a potential solution to the small-scale problems and could explain the observed cored density profiles in dwarf galaxies \citep{Schive2014a,Robles2015,Marsh:2015wka,Schive2016,May2021}. At larger scales, where the de Broglie wavelength is much smaller than the relevant structures, FDM retains the successes of CDM \citep{Du2017, Mocz2017, Zhang2018}. While firm limits have now been placed on models in which FDM makes up the entirety of the dark matter---from observations including the Lyman-$\alpha$ forest \citep{2021PhRvL.126g1302R}, strong gravitational lensing \citep{2022MNRAS.517.1867L,2023MNRAS.524L..84P}, and galaxy abundances \citep{2022MNRAS.510.1425K}---models in which FDM makes up only a fraction of the total may remain viable. Exploring such scenarios will require fast and flexible approaches to modelling FDM physics.

The quantum nature of FDM gives rise to stable, self-gravitating solitonic cores at the centres of halos \citep{Schive2014a,Schwabe2016,Mocz2017,Zhang2018, Ferreira2021}. These solitons, which are non-linear ground-state solutions of the Schr\"odinger-Poisson (SP) equations with characteristic densities and radii set by $m_\mathrm{b}$, provide a natural explanation for the observed cores in dwarf galaxies, circumventing the cusp-core and too-big-to-fail problems of CDM \citep{Robles2019}. FDM halos form an extended envelope with a density profile similar to the NFW profile seen in CDM halos \citep{Navarro1997} outside the soliton. Surrounding the solitonic core, the FDM halo is composed of excited quantum states whose interference generates persistent density fluctuations, or ``granules,'' on scales comparable to the de Broglie wavelength \citep{Hui2017, Schive2014a}.

A typical FDM halo is characterized by order unity density fluctuations. These fluctuations arise from excited states, which produce quantum interference outside the solitonic core. Additionally, these excited modes affect the core itself \citep{Li2021}, through both gravity and quantum pressure gradients, leading to oscillations and random motion that influence nearby structures. These fluctuations not only shape the internal dynamics of the halo but also provide a unique signature distinguishing FDM from CDM. The fluctuations act as a heating source, imparting random velocity kicks to stars and dark matter particles, leading to observable effects such as the thickening of stellar disks \citep{Church2019, El-Zant2020}, the broadening of tidal streams \citep{Amorisco2018, Dalal2021}, and the suppression of dynamical friction, which can prevent the inward migration of black holes and star clusters \citep{Bar-Or2019}.

The computational requirements for simulating full FDM dynamics have resulted in significant limitations in the cosmological volumes that can be studied \citep{Woo2009, Schive2014a, Veltmaat2018, Mocz2020}. While methods that do not account for the full wave dynamics have allowed for simulations with larger volumes, closer to those achievable with traditional N-body and smoothed-particle hydrodynamics (SPH) approaches for CDM \citep{Schive2016, Veltmaat2016, Nori2018, Zhang2018, Nori2019}, these simulations fail to capture inherent wave phenomena such as interference effects. These wave effects can significantly influence the evolution of the system, especially on small scales \citep{Li2019}, raising concerns about the validity of results obtained without solving the fundamental wave equations.

In a study of the effects of granules on particle orbits, \cite{Chowdhury2021} used high-resolution simulations of an FDM halo of mass $6.6 \times 10^9 \, \mathrm{M}_\odot$ with $m_\mathrm{b} = 8 \times 10^{-23} \, \mathrm{eV})$, which was extracted from a larger cosmological volume. \cite{Chowdhury2021} explored a statistical, diffusion model to understand the cumulative impact of granules on particle orbits, and considered only point-like particles (such as stars or black holes), such that their results may not apply to objects with sizes comparable to the scale of the granules, such as subhalos, in which the finite size may significantly influence the magnitude of stochastic perturbations.

In this study, we introduce a semi-analytic model for the impact of FDM granules on subhalos that can overcome many of the limitations of numerical simulations. Instead of a diffusion-based approach, we represent the stochastic perturbations as a Fourier series with random coefficients, allowing us to study their effects on individual orbits rather than just statistical populations. Using numerical simulations from \cite{Chowdhury2021}, we calibrate our model and compare it to the previous methods. We also incorporate finite-size effects to examine how an extended subhalo interacts with the granular FDM environment. To validate our finite-size model, we developed \textit{FDM-simulator}, which is designed to study how subhalos move and interact within a background density field. Our results provide a more detailed understanding of how FDM quantum fluctuations impact subhalo dynamics.

This paper is organized as follows. In Section~\ref{sec:methods}, we briefly review the structure of the FDM halo. We also introduce the Paley-Wiener representation of Brownian motion and describe how it is applied to generate random walks due to granule perturbations. Section~\ref{sec:point_mass} describes the results of our point-mass model. In Section~\ref{sec:finite}, we describe \textit{FDM-Simulator}, which is designed to test and validate our model for perturbations of finite-size subhalos, and introduce our results from the simulation and our semi-analytic model. We summarize and conclude in Section~\ref{sec:conclusions}. Additionally, we compare our model with theoretical expectations from kinetic theory in Appendix~\ref{sec:diffusion}. Appendix~\ref{sec:appendixB} presents a detailed analysis of the statistical properties of granule-induced stochastic forcing, including the acceleration variance and the anisotropy of the resulting velocity dispersion.

\section{Methods}
\label{sec:methods}
\subsection{Fuzzy Dark Matter halo structure}
\label{sec:FDM_structure}

Fuzzy Dark Matter (FDM) halos consist of a central core embedded in an NFW-like density profile. The central core is a stationary, minimum-energy solution of the Schr\"odinger–Poisson equation, called a ``soliton''. Quantum wave interference within an FDM halo generates persistent de Broglie-scale density fluctuations, known as granules. These granules are stochastically distributed and remain undamped over cosmological timescales. As a result, the mass density profiles of dark matter halos exhibit fine-scale fluctuations due to wave interference \citep{Hui2017,Du2017}. These granulations impart a markedly different structure to FDM halos compared to those predicted by cold dark matter (CDM) and warm dark matter (WDM) models \citep{Schive2014a,Schive2016,May2023}.

Outside the soliton, the density fluctuations within an FDM halo can be conceptualized as a sea of quasi-particles possessing an effective mass of:
\begin{equation}
    m_\mathrm{eff} = \rho (f \lambda_\mathrm{db})^3,
    \label{eq:meff}
\end{equation}
where $f=0.282$ for a Gaussian velocity distribution in the halo particles \citep{Bar-Or2019,El-Zant2020,Chiang2021,Chowdhury2021}, $\lambda_\mathrm{db}$ is the de Broglie wavelength, which is given by
\begin{equation}
    \lambda_\mathrm{db} = \frac{\mathrm{h}}{m_\mathrm{b} \sigma_\mathrm{Jeans}},
    \label{eq:Ldb}
\end{equation}
where $\mathrm{h}$ is Planck's constant, $m_\mathrm{b}$ is the mass of the FDM particle, and $\sigma_\mathrm{Jeans}$ is the velocity dispersion in the halo found by solving the Jeans equation. In an FDM halo, self-gravity is counterbalanced by both random motion and quantum pressure (as discussed in \citealt{Hui2017}). When solving the Jeans equation, this results in an ``effective'' velocity dispersion that is greater than the actual dispersion caused solely by random motion. $\sigma_\mathrm{Jeans}$ is obtained by solving the spherical Jeans equation under the assumption of velocity isotropy with the time-averaged density profile of the halo. The velocity dispersion of quasi-particles is the same as the time-and-shell-averaged velocity dispersion $\sigma_\mathrm{h}$, which is approximately equal to $\sigma_\mathrm{Jeans}/\sqrt{2}$ \citep{Chowdhury2021}.

The effective mass of the quasi-particles can be translated into an effective size by equating $m_\mathrm{eff}$ to $\rho V$, where $V = (\pi/6)d_\mathrm{eff}^3$ is the volume of a quasi-particle of diameter $d_\mathrm{eff}$, which is given by $d_\mathrm{eff}\approx 0.35 \lambda_\mathrm{db}$ \citep{Chowdhury2021}. From this, we can estimate that the magnitude of the acceleration due to a nearby quasi-particle will be:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:a_eff_out}
    a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{outskirt} \approx \frac{\mathrm{G} m_\mathrm{eff}}{d_\mathrm{eff}^2}.
\end{equation}

However, this equation is valid only outside the soliton. It breaks down inside the solitonic core, where the wavefunction is more coherent and the density profile is approximately flat. In this regime, we adopt a simplified prescription in which the outskirt contribution is fixed to a constant value equal to $a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{outskirt}(r_\mathrm{sol})$ for $r<r_\mathrm{sol}$, where $r_\mathrm{sol}$ denotes the boundary of the soliton, beyond which the density profile transitions to an NFW-like behavior.

To capture the full structure of the acceleration perturbations, we supplement the outskirt contribution with a separate component arising from the motion of the solitonic core. We define a characteristic core acceleration based on the enclosed solitonic core mass as
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:a_eff_core}
    a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{core} = \frac{GM(r_\mathrm{c})}{r_\mathrm{c}^2},
\end{equation}
which is treated as spatially uniform within the solitonic core (i.e., for $r\lesssim r_\mathrm{c}$, where $r_\mathrm{c}$ is the core radius of the soliton) and set to zero for $r>r_\mathrm{c}$.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/granule_spectrum_density.pdf}
    \caption{Spectral density of the solitonic core and homogeneous field. The blue vertical line shows the frequency of the core oscillation given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:f_granule_sol}. The orange vertical line shows the characteristic spectral width of the homogenous density field, $f_0$, defined as $\sqrt{\mathcal{F}(f_0)/\mathcal{F}(0)}=1/2$. $f_0$ is close to the value given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:f_granule_out} (black vertical line).}
    \label{fig:spectral_density}
\end{figure}

The frequency of the oscillations of the central soliton, which was first derived by \cite{Guzman2004}, is given by \citep[Eq.~4 in ][see \citealt{Veltmaat2016} for more details]{Chowdhury2021}:
\begin{equation}
    f_\mathrm{granule}^\mathrm{soliton} = 10.94\,\hbox{Gyr}^{-1} \left( \frac{\rho_0}{10^9\,\mathrm{M}_\odot \hbox{kpc}^{-3}} \right)^{1/2},
    \label{eq:f_granule_sol}
\end{equation}
where $\rho_0$ is the central density of the FDM halo. This frequency is derived from the quasi-normal mode of the ground state. In contrast, the excited states outside the soliton exhibit higher oscillation frequencies. An approximate estimate of the frequency of the granules in the outskirts can be obtained as follows. We expect the characteristic frequency to scale roughly as $f_\mathrm{granule} \sim \sigma_\mathrm{h}/d_\mathrm{eff}$. Substituting these expressions, we have
 \begin{equation}
 f_\mathrm{granule}^\mathrm{outskirt} = \frac{m_\mathrm{b} \sigma_\mathrm{Jeans}^2}{0.35\sqrt{2}h}.
 \label{eq:f_granule_out}
 \end{equation}

To verify this result for the frequencies of granules, we run two simulations: (1) an isolated soliton with a central density of $10^8 \mathrm{M}_{\odot}/\mathrm{kpc}^3$; (2) a homogenous density field with a velocity dispersion of $17.8\mathrm{km/s}$ to mimic the outskirts of an FDM halo (full details of our simulation methodology are given in Sec.~\ref{sec:FDMSimulator}). We then compute the spectral density as
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}(f)=\frac{1}{V}\int \frac{1}{t_{\mathrm{max}}}\left|\int_0^{t_{\mathrm{max}}} \rho(\mathrm{x},t) e^{i 2 \pi f t} \mathrm{d}t\right|^2 \mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}^3.
\label{eq:sepctral}
\end{equation}
The spectral densities from these two simulations are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:spectral_density}. For the case of a soliton (blue curve), the first peak of the spectrum corresponds to the frequency given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:f_granule_sol} (blue vertical line). For the homogenous case, the spectral density has a bell shape~\footnote{For this case, we subtract the mean density before computing the spectral density.} We can define a characteristic spectral width, $f_0$, as $\sqrt{\mathcal{F}(f_0)/\mathrm{F}(0)}=1/2$ (vertical orange line). $f_0$ is close to the frequency given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:f_granule_out} (black vertical line). We note that while the wavefunction of the granules has a well-defined peak in its spectral density, the density (which is related to the inner product of the wavefunction with itself) does not, due to the non-linear coupling of modes. Nevertheless, the frequency given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:f_granule_out} provides a useful estimate of the typical frequency of oscillations. We further note that had we computed the spectral density of the acceleration field due to granules (instead of their density field), we would have obtained a different estimate of $f_0$ . We find that the precise choice of $f_\mathrm{granule}^\mathrm{outskirt}$ does not significantly affect our results.

\subsection{Using the Paley-Wiener representation of a random walk}
\label{sec:Paley-Wiener}

We consider that granules act as a source of stochastic gravitational accelerations, changing in both time and space, and that these perturbations will cause an orbiting body (e.g. a star, black hole, or subhalo) to experience a random walk in velocity. We use the Paley-Wiener representation of Brownian motion \citep[Eq.~8 in ][]{Higham2015}, in which the coefficients of a Fourier series are random variables drawn from a normal distribution with zero mean. This allows us to generate a random walk in the velocity of the particle as a function of time:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:vx}
    v_x(\tau) =  v_{x,0} \frac{\tau}{\sqrt{2\pi}} + \frac{2}{\sqrt{\pi}} \sum_{n=1}^m v_{x,n} \frac{\sin(n\tau/2)}{n}.
\end{equation}
(for the $x$-component of velocity, with similar expressions for the $y$ and $z$-components), where $v_{x,i}$ are the random coefficients, $\tau$ is a dimensionless time that runs from 0 to $2\pi$, defined as
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:tau}
    \tau = 2 \pi \frac{t}{t_\mathrm{max}},
\end{equation}
where $t_\mathrm{max}$ is the maximum time to which we want to run our calculation, and we choose $m$ such that the frequency of the fastest mode in the Paley-Wiener expansion equals the frequency of the granule structure oscillations\footnote{In the usual Paley-Weiner representation of Brownian motion, $m\rightarrow \infty$. Here, we truncate the series as the FDM granules have a natural frequency above which we expect no fluctuations.}:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:frequency}
    2 \pi = \frac{m}{4} \tau_\mathrm{granule},
\end{equation}
where $\tau_\mathrm{granule}$ is the oscillation period of the granules in the dimensionless time, given by $\tau_\mathrm{granule} = 2\pi/ f_\mathrm{granule} t_\mathrm{max}$. We then find $m$ using
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:m}
    m = 4 f_\mathrm{granule} t_\mathrm{max},
\end{equation}
where $f_\mathrm{granule}$ is given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:f_granule_sol}. The factor of 4 was chosen empirically and found to be sufficient to ensure convergence in the resulting acceleration statistics over the full duration $t_\mathrm{max}$. Since $m$ must be an integer we first compute $m$ using the above for our desired $t_\mathrm{max}$, and then increase $t_\mathrm{max}$ to make $m$ equal to the next largest integer.

To incorporate the effects of the perturbations into particle orbit integrations, using acceleration is more direct than velocity. Therefore, we take the derivative of Eq.~\eqref{eq:vx} with respect to time, finding an acceleration:
\begin{equation}
    \frac{dv_x(\tau)}{d\tau}\frac{d\tau}{dt} = \frac{\sqrt{2\pi}}{t_\mathrm{max}} v_{x,0}+\sum_{n=1}^m \frac{2\sqrt{\pi}}{t_\mathrm{max}} v_{x,n} \cos(n\tau/2).
\end{equation}
This suggests that the standard deviation of the coefficients in the series could be written as:
\begin{equation}
    \frac{2\sqrt{\pi}}{t_\mathrm{max}} v_{x,n} = a_\mathrm{eff} \approx  \frac{\mathrm{G} m_\mathrm{eff}}{d_\mathrm{eff}^2},
\end{equation}
such that the coefficients in the terms in this acceleration series will also be drawn from a normal distribution of zero mean, but with a width
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:sigma_a}
    \sigma_a = a_\mathrm{eff}.
\end{equation}

In practice, the stochastic acceleration field is composed of two physically distinct and statistically independent components: one from the solitonic core and one from the outer halo. These are modeled as separate Fourier series, each sampled from a normal distribution with width
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:sigma_core_out}
\sigma_a^\mathrm{core} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{core}, \quad
\sigma_a^\mathrm{outskirt} = a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{outskirt},
\end{equation}
where $a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{core}$ and $a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{outskirt}$ are defined in Eqs.~\eqref{eq:a_eff_core} and \eqref{eq:a_eff_out}, respectively. These two components are generated independently and subsequently summed to yield the total stochastic acceleration field. The factor $1/\sqrt{3}$ in the core component arises because the solitonic-core-induced acceleration is modeled as isotropic in three dimensions, and $a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{core}$ corresponds to the total RMS amplitude. In contrast, $a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{outskirt}$ corresponds to the RMS value along a single direction and thus does not require the $1/\sqrt{3}$ normalization applied in the isotropic core case.

We can then write the series for the acceleration due to the effects of granules (for the $x$-component of acceleration, with similar expressions for the $y$ and $z$-components) as:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ax}
    a^\mathrm{granule}_x(\tau) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{f_\mathrm{granule}t_\mathrm{max}}}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}a_{x,0} + \sum_{n=1}^m a_{x,n} \cos(n\tau/2)\right),
\end{equation}
where the coefficients $a_{x,n}$ are sampled independently for the core and outskirt components, using normal distributions with widths given by Eqs.~\eqref{eq:sigma_a} and \eqref{eq:sigma_core_out}. The scaling factor $f_\mathrm{granule}$ is likewise component-dependent: for the core, we adopt the constant value in Eq.~\eqref{eq:f_granule_sol}; for the outskirt, $f_\mathrm{granule}$ is fixed to the soliton-boundary value for $r < r_\mathrm{sol}$, and follows Eq.~\eqref{eq:f_granule_out} otherwise. The prefactor $1/\sqrt{f_\mathrm{granule}t_\mathrm{max}}$ ensures that the injected stochastic variance is independent of $t_\mathrm{max}$, consistent with the expected fluctuation timescales derived from a truncated Paley-Wiener expansion of Brownian motion. This procedure yields two independent acceleration fields, $a^\mathrm{granule}_\mathrm{core}$ and $a^\mathrm{granule}_\mathrm{outskirt}$, which are subsequently combined to compute the total stochastic acceleration.

In Appendix~\ref{sec:appendixB} we use \textit{FDM-Simulator} to show that the acceleration variance due to granules is both velocity-dependent and anisotropic. To model these effects, we introduce velocity-dependent scaling factors, $A_\parallel$ and $A_\perp$, corresponding to the components of the acceleration parallel and perpendicular to the particle's instantaneous velocity. These factors encapsulate how the particle's motion influences the coherence time and magnitude of granule interactions. Specifically, the outskirt acceleration $a^\mathrm{granule}_\mathrm{outskirt}$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:ax} is rescaled by dividing the parallel and perpendicular components by $\sqrt{A_\parallel}$ and $\sqrt{A_\perp}$, respectively. The modified acceleration becomes:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ax_scaled}
\mathbfit{a}^\mathrm{granule}_\mathrm{outskirt,scaled} = \frac{\mathbfit{a}^\mathrm{granule}_\mathrm{outskirt,\parallel}}{\sqrt{A_\parallel}} + \frac{\mathbfit{a}^\mathrm{granule}_\mathrm{outskirt,\perp}}{\sqrt{A_\perp}},
\end{equation}
where $\mathbfit{a}^\mathrm{granule}_\mathrm{outskirt,\parallel} = (\mathbfit{a}^\mathrm{granule}_\mathrm{outskirt} \cdot \hat{v})\hat{v}$ and $\mathbfit{a}^\mathrm{granule}_\mathrm{outskirt,\perp} = \mathbfit{a}^\mathrm{granule}_\mathrm{outskirt}-\mathbfit{a}_\parallel$, and $\hat{v}$ is the unit vector in the direction of the particle's velocity. Note that this velocity-dependent rescaling applies only to $\mathbfit{a}^\mathrm{granule}_\mathrm{outskirt}$, and is not used for the core-induced component. The scaling factors $A_\parallel$ and $A_\perp$ are functions of velocity magnitude $v$ and background velocity dispersion $\sigma_J$, as given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:Atotal, Aperp} and Eq.\eqref{eq:Aparallel}.

\section{Point Mass object}
\label{sec:point_mass}

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/a_eff.pdf}
    \caption{The structure of the FDM halo considered in this work. The top panel shows the density profile $\rho(r)$. The density profile exhibits a soliton-like structure at small radii, extending out to approximately $r_\mathrm{sol} = 2.7\,r_\mathrm{c}$ (dashed green vertical line), where $r_\mathrm{c} = 0.72\,\mathrm{kpc}$ is the core radius of the soliton (dashed red vertical line). Beyond $r_\mathrm{sol}$, the profile transitions to an NFW-like structure at larger radii. The radius $r_\mathrm{sol}$ is considered to be the boundary of the soliton, marking the approximate point where the soliton ends and the outer halo begins. The middle panel shows the de Broglie wavelength $\lambda_\mathrm{db}$ as a function of radius $r$. The bottom panel presents the outskirt contribution to the acceleration, $a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{outskirt}$, which is set to a constant value equal to $a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{outskirt}(r_\mathrm{sol})$ within the soliton, and decreases beyond $r_\mathrm{sol}$.}
    \label{fig:halo_structure}
\end{figure}

As previously mentioned, the granules are variations in the density arising from the quantum nature of fuzzy dark matter, which will cause random perturbations to subhalo orbits. \cite{Chowdhury2021} model the effects of these perturbations through direct simulation and as a diffusion process. Throughout that paper, they simulated the impact of the perturbations in a single FDM halo of viral mass $M_\mathrm{vir} = 6.6\times10^9 \mathrm{M}_\odot$. The FDM halo is extracted from a cosmological simulation with a bosonic dark matter particle mass of $m_\mathrm{b} = 8\times10^{-23}\mathrm{eV}$ \citep{Schive2014b}.  Here, we apply our model for the acceleration derived from the Paley-Wiener representation of Brownian motion (Eq.~\ref{eq:ax}) to model the effects of granule perturbations. To compare with the results of \cite{Chowdhury2021}, we use the same density profile as in that paper, also represented in the top panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:halo_structure}. The density profile of the FDM halo is given by:
\begin{equation}
    \rho(r)=\left\{\begin{array}{rll}
\rho_\mathrm{sol}(r) = & \frac{\rho_\mathrm{c}}{(1+0.091(r/r_c)^2)^8}, & \hbox{for } r\leq r_\mathrm{sol}, \\
\rho_\mathrm{nfw}(r) = & \frac{\rho_\mathrm{s}}{(r/r_s)(1+r/r_s)^2}, & \hbox{for } r>r_\mathrm{sol},
    \end{array}\right.
    \label{eq:density_profile}
\end{equation}
where $\rho_\mathrm{c}$ is the central density and $r_\mathrm{c}$ is the core radius, defined by $\rho_\mathrm{sol}(r_\mathrm{c}) = \rho_\mathrm{c}/2.$ The core radius is $r_\mathrm{c} = 0.72\,\mathrm{kpc}$, obtained from the scaling relation:
\begin{equation}
    \left(\frac{r_\mathrm{c}}{\mathrm{kpc}}\right)^4=\frac{1.954\times10^9\mathrm{M}_\odot\mathrm{kpc}^{-3}}{\rho_0}\left(\frac{m_\mathrm{b}}{10^{-23}\mathrm{eV}}\right)^{-2},
\end{equation}
which arises from the scaling symmetry of the Schr\"odinger-Poisson (SP) equation \citep{Seidel1990,Guzman2006}. We consider $r_\mathrm{sol} = 2.7r_\mathrm{c}$, which is the approximate radius at which the density profile begins to diverge from the soliton structure, transitioning to an NFW-like profile, at the boundary of the soliton. Recent studies, such as \cite{Chan2022}, find that most haloes have transition radii $r_\mathrm{sol}\leq 3r_\mathrm{c}$ and similarly, \cite{Yavetz2022} report even smaller values (e.g., $r_\mathrm{sol}\approx2r_\mathrm{c}$). Theoretically, ensuring a smooth transition from the solitonic core to an NFW profile requires continuity in both the density and its first derivative, implying that $r_\mathrm{sol}\leq 3r_\mathrm{c}$ is required. Since the simulation model we currently use for comparison follows the approach of \cite{Schive2014a}, our choice of $r_\mathrm{sol} = 2.7r_\mathrm{c}$ is consistent. Inside the soliton, quantum pressure dominates, while in the outer parts of the halo, gravity dominates and the formation of small-scale density fluctuations occurs \citep{Chan2022}. For the NFW-like density profile, the scale density $\rho_\mathrm{s} = 5.5\times10^5\,\mathrm{M}_\odot\mathrm{kpc}^{-3}$ can be obtained from the continuity condition for the density, with the scale radius $r_\mathrm{s} = 10\,\mathrm{kpc}$ \citep[see][for further details]{Schive2014b,Chowdhury2021}.

Using the density profile shown above, the de Broglie wavelength and the acceleration due to a nearby quasi-particle in this FDM halo can be computed by the method derived in Section~\ref{sec:FDM_structure}, and are shown in the middle and bottom panels of Fig.~\ref{fig:halo_structure} respectively. The middle panel presents the de Broglie wavelength $\lambda_\mathrm{db}$ as a function of $r$, given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:Ldb}. The blue curve shows how $\lambda_\mathrm{db}$ varies with distance from the centre, reflecting the quantum nature within the FDM halo. The bottom panel shows the outskirt contribution to the granule-induced acceleration, $a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{outskirt}$, as described by Eq.~\eqref{eq:a_eff_out}. Within the soliton ($r<r_\mathrm{sol}$), this quantity is fixed to the boundary value $a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{outskirt} (r_\mathrm{sol})$, reflecting the coherent nature of the core. At larger radii, $a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{outskirt}$  decreases, and the influence of granules diminishes. In addition to this outskirt term, the solitonic core also contributes a constant core component, given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:a_eff_core}. Based on our simulations, we estimate the amplitude of this contribution to be approximately $a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{core} (r\lesssim r_\mathrm{c}) \sim 530.4\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}\,Gyr^{-1}}$.

To investigate the orbital evolution of an object within an FDM halo, we account for three sources of acceleration: first, the smooth acceleration,
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ag}
    a_\mathrm{smooth} = \frac{\mathrm{G}M(r)}{r^2},
\end{equation}
directed toward the centre of the halo, where $M(r)$ is the time-and-shell averaged enclosed mass at radius $r$; second, the acceleration due to the random perturbation as Eq.~\eqref{eq:ax}; and third, the effect of dynamical friction, which we now describe.

Though FDM seems promising in explaining several small-scale observational inconsistencies with CDM theory, it can drastically change the dynamics of galaxies as compared to CDM. Some ways in which galactic dynamics in FDM vary most strongly from CDM are dynamical heating and friction due to the wave-like substructure of FDM. We consider the classical treatment of dynamical friction---see more details in Section 3.1 of \cite{Lancaster2020} and \cite{Chowdhury2021}---which was first derived by \cite{Chandrasekhar1943}.

Assuming the background to be an infinite medium composed of `field' particles, whose distribution is homogeneous in position space and isotropic in velocity space, we model the cumulative effect of many two-body interactions between the field particles and the satellite mass, under the assumption that the satellite mass significantly exceeds that of the field particles. These approximations provide a framework for estimating the diffusion of the satellite or subject particle through phase space.

The dynamical friction coefficient, also known as the first-order diffusion coefficient parallel to the velocity of the particle $v$, is given by
\begin{equation}
    D[\Delta v_\parallel] = -\frac{4\pi^2\mathrm{G}^2M_\mathrm{particle}\rho \ln \Lambda_\mathrm{FDM}}{\sigma_\mathrm{Jeans}^2}\mathbb{G}(X),
    \label{eq:D_first}
\end{equation}
where $\rho$ and $\sigma_\mathrm{h}$ are the time-and-shell-averaged density and velocity dispersion, which is approximately equal to $\sigma_\mathrm{Jeans}/\sqrt{2}$ (the same as that of the quasiparticles), $M_\mathrm{particle}$ is the mass of the subhalo, and $\mathbb{G}(X)$ is given by
\begin{equation}
    \mathbb{G}(X) = \frac{1}{2X^2}\left[\mathrm{erf}(X)-\frac{2X}{\sqrt{\pi}}\mathrm{e}^{-X^2}\right],
    \label{eq:GX}
\end{equation}
where $X \equiv v/\sqrt{2}\sigma_\mathrm{Jeans}$, and $\mathrm{erf}(X)$ is the error function.
Here the Coulomb logarithm is defined as
\begin{equation}
\ln{\Lambda_\mathrm{FDM}} = \ln{\frac{4\pi r}{\lambda_\mathrm{db}}},
\label{eq:L_FDM}
\end{equation}
where $\lambda_\mathrm{db}$ is de Broglie wavelength, given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:Ldb}, and $r$ is the distance from the soliton centre. The classical treatment of dynamical friction assumes that the medium through which the satellite travels is infinite and that the satellite is a point mass.

We can then use the above equations to relate the dynamical friction force, directed anti-parallel to the velocity of the orbiting body, to the diffusion coefficient as
\begin{equation}
    F_\mathrm{DF} = \frac{8\pi^2\mathrm{G}^2M_\mathrm{particle}^2\rho \ln \Lambda_\mathrm{FDM}}{v^2}\mathbb{G}(X).
\end{equation}
Then the acceleration due to the dynamical friction is given by
\begin{equation}
    \mathbfit{a}^\mathrm{DF} = \frac{\mathbfit{F}_\mathrm{DF}}{M_\mathrm{particle}},
    \label{eq:a_DF}
\end{equation}
which is directly opposite to the particle's velocity, resulting in a deceleration of its motion.

However, the ``standard'' Chandrasekhar treatment is known to fail in reproducing the effect of stalling in cored density distributions \citep{Read2006,Kaur2018,Banik2021}. To address this limitation, we improved our dynamical friction calculation by adopting the model introduced by \cite{Kaur2018}, which explicitly accounts for the core-stalling effect.

During the motion of the particle, we sum these three contributions to the acceleration: the smooth acceleration given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:ag}, the acceleration of the random perturbation, and the acceleration due to the dynamical friction given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:a_DF}. The granule-induced perturbations consist of two statistically independent components: one from the solitonic core and one from the outer halo. Both are generated using the Fourier series described in Eq.~\eqref{eq:ax}, with sampling widths determined by Eq.~\eqref{eq:sigma_core_out}. The outskirt component is subsequently modified by a velocity-dependent rescaling following Eq.~\eqref{eq:ax_scaled}. Then the $x$-component of acceleration is given by:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:a_pm}
    a_x = a^\mathrm{smooth}_x + \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\alpha_\mathrm{core} a^\mathrm{granule}_{\mathrm{core},x} + \alpha_\mathrm{outer} a^\mathrm{granule}_{\mathrm{outskirt},\mathrm{scaled},x} + \beta a^\mathrm{DF}_x,
\end{equation}
where $\alpha_\mathrm{core}$ and $\alpha_\mathrm{outer}$ are dimensionless scaling parameters for the core and outskirt components, respectively. The value of $\alpha_\mathrm{core}$ would be of order unity if the stochastic acceleration were dominated by the influence of a single granule, as expected in the coherent regime of the solitonic core. Outside the soliton, since multiple granules contribute, their accelerations partially cancel out. We therefore introduce a $\alpha_\mathrm{outer}$ that accounts for the fact that an orbiting object interacts simultaneously with many granules, and we calibrate its value to match the results of numerical simulations. Similarly, $\beta$ is a dimensionless parameter to calibrate the strength of dynamical friction.

By integrating the above formula for acceleration over time, it is possible to investigate the orbital evolution of an object in an FDM halo. At $t = 0$, we initialize the simulation by placing a single particle at a radial distance $r_0 = 0.1\,\mathrm{kpc}$ from the centre of the FDM halo, with an initial velocity determined by the circular velocity at that radius, given by $v_0 = \sqrt{\mathrm{G}M_\mathrm{r_0}/r_0}$, where $M_\mathrm{r_0}$ is the enclosed mass at $r_0$, and directed perpendicular to the radial vector resulting in an initially circular orbit. The object is then evolved for a maximum time of $25\,\mathrm{Gyr}$, using a time step of $\Delta t = 0.001\,\mathrm{Gyr}$. At each time step, the acceleration acting on the particle, given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:a_pm}, is computed as the sum of four components: (1) the smooth acceleration, (2) a stochastic acceleration from solitonic core fluctuations, (3) a velocity-dependent rescaling stochastic acceleration from outer-halo granules, and (4) the dynamical friction caused by interactions with the background field. Both stochastic components are initially modeled using a truncated Paley-Wiener representation of Brownian motion.

As described above, the acceleration due to the effects of granules is modeled using two statistically independent components: one from the solitonic core and one from the outer halo. Each is realized as a separate Fourier series, with coefficients $a_{x,n}$ drawn from normal distributions with zero mean and widths, $\sigma_a^\mathrm{core}$ and $\sigma_a^\mathrm{outskirt}$, given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:sigma_core_out}. At each time step, the values of $a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{core}(r)$ and $a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{outskirt}(r)$ are evaluated at the particle’s current position, and used to compute the corresponding widths. 

We sample the Fourier coefficients from normal distributions with zero mean and unit width, and scale them by the appropriate combination of $\alpha$ and $a_\mathrm{eff}$: specifically, we use $\alpha_\mathrm{core} a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{core}/\sqrt{3}$ for the solitonic core component, and $\alpha_\mathrm{outer} a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{outskirt}$ for the outer halo component. This scaling must be applied at each time step, as both $a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{core}$ and $a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{outskirt}$ can only be determined once the integration reaches that timestep.

At each timestep, we also determine the velocity-dependent factors $A_\parallel$ and $A_\perp$, using the particle's velocity $v$ and the background velocity dispersion $\sigma_J(r)$, according to Eq.~\eqref{eq:Atotal, Aperp} and Eq.\eqref{eq:Aparallel}. These are then used to rescale the parallel and perpendicular components of the acceleration, following Eq.~\eqref{eq:ax_scaled}, to incorporate the anisotropic response to granule perturbations observed in simulations. The object's position and velocity are updated iteratively using these accelerations, and its trajectory is tracked over time. This approach allows us to study granules' influence on the object's orbital evolution in an FDM halo.

We search for the parameters, $\alpha_\mathrm{core}$, $\alpha_\mathrm{outer}$ and $\beta$, which result in the best match with the simulation data from \cite{Chowdhury2021} by minimizing the figure-of-merit function
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:FoM}
    \chi^2 = \sum\limits_{i=1}\limits^{N_{\rm steps}}\left(\ln{r_i}-\ln{r_i^{\rm sim}}\right)^2,
\end{equation}
where $N_{\rm steps}$ is the total number of timesteps in the simulation. At each timestep, we record the radius $r_i$ predicted by our model and compare it to $r_i^{\rm sim}$, the corresponding value from the numerical simulation. Since we run multiple realizations, $r_i$ represents the mean radius over all realizations at timestep $i$.

\begin{figure*}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/ReproduceChowdhury.pdf}
    \caption{The orbital evolution of an object in an FDM halo. Each row corresponds to a different particle mass, from top to bottom: massless particles, $1\times10^5 \mathrm{M}_\odot$, $1\times10^6 \mathrm{M}_\odot$, $5\times10^6 \mathrm{M}_\odot$ and $1\times10^7 \mathrm{M}_\odot$ as indicate in the right-hand panels. In the left panels, solid lines with different colours show the median radius of the particles over time, along with the 16th--84th percentile variation in radius, using our semi-analytic model for point-mass objects. For various particle masses, we calibrated a consistent scaling parameter, $\alpha_\mathrm{core} = 0.3$, $\alpha_\mathrm{outer} = 5.0$, to adjust the granule acceleration, and $\beta = 0.16$, to scale the dynamical friction effects, comparing our semi-analytic model with numerical simulation (green lines). The right panels display the velocity dispersion $\sigma$.}
    \label{fig:ReproduceChowdhury}
\end{figure*}

The results for various particle masses are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:ReproduceChowdhury}. We find that $\alpha_\mathrm{core} = 0.3$, $\alpha_\mathrm{outer} = 5.0$ and $\beta = 0.16$ result in an excellent match of our semi-analytic model with the results of the numerical simulation. Notably, the adopted value $\alpha_\mathrm{outer} = 5.0$ is consistent with the range inferred from our \textit{FDM-Simulator} analysis as we will show in section \ref{sec:finite_size_calibration}.

In the left panels of Fig.~\ref{fig:ReproduceChowdhury}, from top to bottom, the solid curves with various colours show the evolution of the median distance from the soliton centre over time, calculated from 100 realizations, using our semi-analytic point-mass model, for masses $\left\{\mathrm{massless},\,1\times10^5 \mathrm{M}_\odot,\,1\times10^6 \mathrm{M}_\odot,\,5\times10^6 \mathrm{M}_\odot,\,1\times10^7 \mathrm{M}_\odot \right\}$. In comparison, the green lines show the results of the numerical simulation. In each case, the shaded region with the same colour highlights the $16^\mathrm{th}$--$84^\mathrm{th}$ percentile variation in $r$.

In the absence of dynamical friction, massless particles experience continuous heating from FDM potential fluctuations, causing their orbital radius to increase over time on average. For massive particles, the radius increases until the heating rate from FDM fluctuations balances the cooling rate due to dynamical friction, reaching an equilibrium where the radius, $r$, and velocity dispersion, $\sigma$, stabilize. 

For particles with masses $M = 10^5\,\mathrm{M}_\odot$ and $M = 10^6\,\mathrm{M}_\odot$, the heating rate dominates. In contrast, for $M = 5\times10^6\,\mathrm{M}_\odot$ and $M = 10^7\,\mathrm{M}_\odot$ dynamical friction becomes significant. For $M = 10^7\,\mathrm{M}_\odot$, the balance between heating and cooling results in a slight decrease in $r$ at late times in the simulations of \cite{Chowdhury2021}. This decay is likely due to the gradual increase in the mass of the soliton over time through gravitational cooling. As the soliton gains mass, its random motion weakens, reducing the heating effect and causing the equilibrium radius to shrink slowly. Our model does not include this gravitational cooling effect, so we do not see the decrease in $r$ at late times.

The solid curves in the right-hand panels show the particle ensemble's 1D velocity dispersion, $\sigma$. Here we compute the velocity dispersion in the $x$-direction (similar results are found for the velocity dispersion in the $y$ and $z$-directions):
\begin{equation}
    \sigma_x = \langle(\Delta v)^2\rangle = \frac{1}{N}\sum^N_{i=1}{(\Delta v_i)^2},
\end{equation}
where $\Delta v_i = v_i - \bar{v}$, $v_i$ is the velocity over different realizations and $\bar{v}$ is the mean velocity. Then the velocity dispersion is $\sigma = \sqrt{(\sigma_x^2+\sigma_y^2+\sigma_z^2)/3}$. The evolution of $\sigma$ shows minimal mass dependence. Initially, $\sigma$ increases rapidly, as particles start with zero velocity relative to the soliton. After this phase, $\sigma$ evolves only slowly.

By parametrizing the granule acceleration and dynamical friction through constant $\alpha$ and $\beta$, we can account for a wide range of particle masses without over-complicating the underlying framework. Our semi-analytic model integrates a particle orbit in around 1 minute, allowing for a much faster parameter space exploration than a full numerical simulation.

\section{Finite size objects}
\label{sec:finite}

We have developed our semi-analytic model for point-mass objects. However, for extended objects, such as subhalos, their spatial extent and structure may significantly influence the stochastic perturbations that they experience.

For an extended object, if its size exceeds the typical size and spacing of granules, the strength of perturbations from those granules that it encompasses will be significantly reduced---it will effectively smooth over some larger region containing many granules, resulting in the acceleration due to granules having a lower amplitude.

\subsection{FDM Simulator}
\label{sec:FDMSimulator}

To validate that our finite-size model is physically accurate and reliable, we test it against direct FDM simulations. While the model captures the main statistical properties of granule-induced fluctuations, their interactions with extended objects can be more complex than analytic approaches assume. Simulations are therefore essential to account for the finite-size effects of FDM granules on extended subhalos.

We simulate the motion of subhalos within a background density field using $\textit{FDM-Simulator}$, a pseudo-spectral code for FDM simulations~\citep{Du:2018qor}. The initial background field is created as a superposition of random waves with a specified momentum distribution $f(k) \equiv |\psi(k)|^2$~\citep{Bar-Or2019,Lancaster2020,Buehler:2022tmr}:
\begin{equation}
\psi(\mathbf{x},0)=\int \mathrm{d}^3 \, \mathbf{k} \psi(\mathbf{k}) e^{i[\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{x}+\phi(\mathbf{k})]},
\label{eq:psi_bg}
\end{equation}
where $\phi(\mathbf{k})$ is a random phase uniformly drawn from $[0,2\pi)$ for each $\mathbf{k}$ mode. The background density is $\rho(\mathbf{x})=\rho_0|\psi(\mathbf{x})|^2$ with $\rho_0$ being the mean density. It is found in cosmological simulations that in a virialized FDM halo $f(k)$ is roughly given by a Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution~\citep{Veltmaat2018}
\begin{align}
4 \pi k^2 f(k) \mathrm{d}k & = 4 \pi v^2 f(v) \mathrm{d}v \nonumber \\
& = 4 \pi v^2 \left(\frac{1}{2\pi \sigma_{\mathrm{Jeans}}^2}\right)^{3/2} \exp \left(-\frac{v^2}{2\sigma_{\mathrm{Jeans}}^2}\right)\mathrm{d} v,
\label{eq:f_k}
\end{align}
where $v=\hbar k/m_\mathrm{b}$, and $\sigma_{\mathrm{Jeans}}$ is the velocity dispersion of the halo. Therefore, we also assume a Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution for the background field. The subhalo is modelled as a static NFW potential. Its acceleration is computed by integrating the acceleration from the background over this NFW profile:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{a}(t)=\int \rho(\mathbf{x},t)\frac{\mathrm{G} M_\mathrm{NFW}(|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_{\rm sub}(t)|)}{|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_{\rm sub}(t)|^2+r_{\rm softening}^2}\frac{\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_{\rm sub}(t)}{|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_{\rm sub}(t)|} \mathrm{d}^3 \mathbf{x}.
\label{eq:a_sub}
\end{equation}
Here $\rho$ is the background density, $M_{\rm NFW}(r)$ is the enclosed mass of an NFW halo, $r_{\rm softening}$ is the softening length, and $\mathbf{x}_{\rm sub}$ is the position of the subhalo.

To study the effects of granules on the orbital evolution of the subhalo in an FDM halo, we focus exclusively on the acceleration caused by interactions with granules. For simplicity, we assume a uniform (apart from granule fluctuations) background such that there is no orbital acceleration, and ignore the effects of dynamical friction in the simulation (by disabling the gravitational back-reaction of the subhalo on the background matter).
We also switch off the self-gravity of the background field to avoid possible nucleation in the background by kinetic relaxation~\citep{Levkov:2018kau,Chen:2020cef}.

In the simulation, we can follow the motion of multiple subhalos at once. These subhalos are placed randomly in the background field and move around due to interactions with the granule structures in the field. Importantly, the subhalos do not interact with each other---their motion is entirely driven by the background. By tracking many subhalos simultaneously, we can measure statistical properties such as the root mean square velocity ($V_\mathrm{rms}$) much faster than running individual simulations for each subhalo. However, because the simulation box is finite, the motion of subhalos will be slightly correlated, and different realizations of the background field can lead to slight variations in the results. For example, the $V_\mathrm{rms}$ can differ by about 7 percent depending on the specific background field used. This variation is similar to that we find when changing the mass or size of the subhalos, so it is essential to run multiple realizations to get reliable results. After testing, we found that running 20 realizations, each with 250 subhalos, is enough to get consistent results for $V_\mathrm{rms}$.

In the simulation, we assume a boson mass $m_\mathrm{b} = 8\times10^{-23}\,\mathrm{eV}$. For the background, we use a host FDM halo with $M_\mathrm{host} = 10^{10}\,\mathrm{M}_\odot$, which is a typical FDM halo mass scale, to estimate relevant densities. Specifically, we set the homogeneous density background as the value $\rho_0 = 1.628\times10^{4}\,\mathrm{M}_\odot/\mathrm{kpc}^3$ at half of the virial radius, $r = 0.5\times R_\mathrm{host} = 28.35\,\mathrm{kpc}$ of the host FDM halo, with the velocity dispersion set to $\sigma_{\mathrm{Jeans}} = 17.775\,\mathrm{km/s}$, resulting in $d_\mathrm{eff}$ set to $2.965\,\mathrm{kpc}$. Using this same background field, we simulate subhalos with various masses, from  $3\times10^5\,\mathrm{M}_\odot$ to $9\times10^8\,\mathrm{M}_\odot$, associated with various half radii $r_{\rm half}$, from 0.322 kpc to 6.317 kpc, which is the radius enclosing half of the total mass of the subhalo. We simulate 250 subhalos with the same mass in each realization and run 20 realizations for each subhalo mass.

