    \label{fig:ReproduceChowdhury_diffusion}
\end{figure*}

Here we compare our semi-analytic model with theoretical prediction based on kinetic theory. As mentioned in Section~\ref{sec:FDM_structure}, the density fluctuations outside the soliton within an FDM halo can be conceptualized as a sea of quasi-particles. Here, the diffusion coefficients are derived by assuming an infinite, homogeneous sea of quasi-particles, in which the unperturbed trajectory of the object is a straight line. The evolution of the energy of a particle can be described by the energy diffusion coefficient, $D[\Delta E]$, see more details in \cite{Bar-Or2019, El-Zant2020, Lancaster2020, Chavanis2021, Chowdhury2021}. The first order diffusion coefficient was given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:D_first}. The second-order coefficient $D[\Delta E]$, which describes the effect of the granules, is given by
\begin{equation}
    D[\Delta E]_\mathrm{2nd\,order} = \frac{1}{2}D[(\Delta v_\parallel)^2] + \frac{1}{2}D[(\Delta v_\perp)^2],
\end{equation}
where $v$ is the velocity of the particle, $D[(\Delta v_\parallel)^2]$ and $D[(\Delta v_\perp)^2]$ are the second-order velocity diffusion coefficients parallel and perpendicular to $v$, given by
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:diff_paral}
    D[(\Delta v_\parallel)^2] = \mathcal{D} \frac{G(X_\mathrm{eff})}{X_\mathrm{eff}},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:diff_perp}
    D[(\Delta v_\perp)^2] = \mathcal{D} \frac{\mathrm{erf}(X_\mathrm{eff})-G(X_\mathrm{eff})}{X_\mathrm{eff}},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{D}=\frac{4\sqrt{2}\pi G^2 \rho m_\mathrm{eff}}{\sigma_\mathrm{h}}\ln{\Lambda_\mathrm{FDM}},
\end{equation}
$m_\mathrm{eff}$ is the effective mass of the quasi-particles, which is given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:meff}, and the Coulomb logarithm $\Lambda_\mathrm{FDM}$ is given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:L_FDM}. Here, $\rho$ and $\sigma_\mathrm{h}$ are the time-and-shell-averaged density and velocity dispersion, the same as velocity dispersion of the quasi-particles, which is approximately equal to $\sigma_\mathrm{Jeans}/\sqrt{2}$, $r$ is the distance from the soliton centre, and $\mathbb{G}(X)$ is given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:GX}, where $X_\mathrm{eff} = v/\sqrt{2}\sigma_\mathrm{h}$.

We consider a discrete random walk process to model the stochastic velocity perturbations caused by granule interactions. Each ``step'' corresponds to a velocity kick imparted by a granule over its characteristic timescale $t_\mathrm{granule} = 1/f_\mathrm{granule}$. The velocity change per step is $\Delta t \approx a_\mathrm{granule}t_\mathrm{granule}$, where $a_\mathrm{granule}$ is the granule acceleration. After $m$ steps, the mean squared velocity change will therefore be $\overline{v^2} = m (a_\mathrm{granule} t_\mathrm{granule})^2$. In the continuum limit, this simplifies to:
\begin{equation}
    \overline{v^2} =  a_\mathrm{granule}^2 t_\mathrm{granule} ( m t_\mathrm{granule}) =  a_\mathrm{granule}^2 t_\mathrm{granule} t.
\end{equation}
For a diffusion process governed by a second-order diffusion coefficient $D[\Delta E]_\mathrm{2nd\,order}$, the mean squared velocity change is defined as $\overline{v^2} = D[\Delta E]_\mathrm{2nd\,order}t$. Therefore we can make the correspondence, $a_\mathrm{granule} = \sqrt\frac{D[\Delta E]_\mathrm{2nd\,order}}{t_\mathrm{granule}}$.
This relation bridges our random walk model to the continuum diffusion framework.

To test consistency, we compute $a_\mathrm{granule}$ from both our semi-analytic model and the diffusion coefficient derived from kinetic theory. These expressions have similar dependencies on the system's physical parameters, but differ in their normalizations, so the random walk coefficients in our Fourier-based framework can be parameterized similarly to our point-mass model. Based on the discussion in Section~\ref{sec:Paley-Wiener}, we also use Eq.~\eqref{eq:ax} as the series for the acceleration due to the effects of granules $a_x^\mathrm{granule}(\tau)$ (for the $x$-component of acceleration, with similar expressions for the $y$ and $z$-components), where we can choose the $a_{x,n}$ coefficients by sampling random numbers from a normal distribution with mean zero and width as $\sigma_a = \sqrt{D[\Delta E]_\mathrm{2nd\,order} t_\mathrm{granule}}$.

However, the velocity diffusion coefficients, given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:diff_paral} and Eq.~\eqref{eq:diff_perp}, are derived under the assumption that velocity diffusion is mainly caused by many weak interactions with quasiparticles. Because of this, these equations are only valid well outside the soliton. Near the soliton, this assumption no longer holds, since the soliton itself acts as a single strong quasiparticle dominating the perturbations. To resolve this issue, \cite{Chowdhury2021} introduce an ``effective model'', in which the diffusion coefficients within a certain radius, $r_\mathrm{cut}$, are replaced by their values at $r_\mathrm{cut}$. They found through trial and error that setting $r_\mathrm{cut} \approx 2.3 r_\mathrm{c}$ produced good agreement with simulation results, especially for low-mass particles. For consistency and to compare with their results, we adopt the same ``effective model'' and use their value of $r_\mathrm{cut}$ in our analysis. 

We then compute the orbital evolution of the particle in the FDM halo. In addition to the stochastic granule-induced perturbations, we also include the smooth acceleration $a^\mathrm{smooth}_x$ and the dynamical friction term $a^\mathrm{DF}_x$ in the equation of motion. During motion, the acceleration acting on the particle is given by 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:a_diffusion}
    a_x = a^\mathrm{smooth}_x + \alpha a^\mathrm{granule}_{x} + \beta a^\mathrm{DF}_x,
\end{equation}
where $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are the calibrated scaling factors. Here we also consider two separate scaling parameters for the granule acceleration, $\alpha_\mathrm{core}$ for $r \leq r_\mathrm{c}$ and $\alpha_\mathrm{outer}$ for $r > r_\mathrm{c}$. Note that, here, we choose to make the transition between $\alpha_\mathrm{core}$ and $\alpha_\mathrm{outer}$ at $r_\mathrm{c}$. This choice is consistent with the definition of $a_\mathrm{eff}^\mathrm{core}$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:a_eff_core}, and reflects the transition between the inner region, where the soliton acts as a coherent perturber dominating the dynamics, and the outer region, where velocity diffusion is driven by cumulative weak interactions with quasiparticles.

In the left panels of Fig.~\ref{fig:ReproduceChowdhury_diffusion}, the solid curves with various colours show the evolution of the median distance from the soliton centre over time, calculated from 100 realizations, using the diffusion method, for various particle masses, while the green lines show the results of the numerical simulation. The right panels show the velocity dispersion.

Using this diffusion approach, we find that the scaling parameters of granule acceleration are calibrated as $\alpha_\mathrm{core} = 5.0$ and $\alpha_\mathrm{outer} = 0.4$. Our value of $\alpha_\mathrm{outer}$ is much smaller than that we obtained in the point-mass model, reflecting the influence of the ``effective model''. We find the best scaling parameter of dynamical friction is $\beta = 0.2$.

\section{Statistical Properties of Granule Forcing}
\label{sec:appendixB}

\subsection{Velocity Variance Induced by Granule Fluctuations}
\label{subsec:acc_point_mass}
Consider a point mass moving through a statistically homogeneous medium composed of FDM particles. Its velocity variance induced by the granule structure can be computed as~\citep{El-Zant:2016byp,Marsh:2018zyw,Bar-Or2019,El-Zant2020}
\begin{equation}
\sigma^2\equiv\langle (\Delta v)^2 \rangle = 2\int_0^t (t-t') \langle \mathbf{a}(\mathbf{x}=0,t=0) \mathbf{a}(\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{v}\,t', t') \rangle dt'.
\label{eq:sigma_pm}
\end{equation}
Here $\mathbf{a}(\mathbf{x},t)$ is the acceleration field, and $\mathbf{v}$ is the velocity of the point mass. Therefore, $\sigma$ depends both on the spatial correlation and on the time correlation of the acceleration field, which can be defined as
\begin{equation}
C_a(\mathbf{x},t) \equiv \langle \mathbf{a}(0,0) \mathbf{a}(\mathbf{x}, t) \rangle.
\label{eq:C_a_x_t}
\end{equation}

To compute the $C(\mathbf{x},t)$, we assume the wavefunction of the homogeneous medium can be treated as a complex Gaussian random field:
\begin{equation}
\psi(\mathbf{x},0)=\int \mathrm{d}^3 \mathbf{k} \psi(\mathbf{k}) e^{i[\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{x}+\phi(\mathbf{k})]},
\label{eq:psi_homo}
\end{equation}
where $\psi(\mathbf{k}$) is the Fourier transform of the wave function, and $\phi$ is a random phase. The evolution of the wavefunction is governed by the Schr\"odinger-Poisson (SP) equations:
\begin{align}
i \hbar \partial_t\psi(\mathbf{x},t)&=-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m_b}\nabla^2\psi(\mathbf{x},t)+m_b\Phi(\mathbf{x},t)\psi(\mathbf{x},t),
\label{eq:schrodinger}
\\
\nabla^2\Phi(\mathbf{x},t)&=4\pi G \left(|\psi(\mathbf{x},t)|^2-\overline{\rho}\right),
\label{eq:poisson}
\end{align}
where $\Phi$ is the gravitational potential and $\overline{\rho}\equiv\overline{|\psi|^2}$ is the mean density. Here we have assumed periodic boundary conditions. For convenience, we introduce dimensionless quantities:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{x}= x \frac{m_b v_0}{\hbar},\quad \tilde{t}=t \frac{m_b v_0^2}{\hbar},\quad
\tilde{\psi}=\psi \frac{\hbar}{m_b v_0^2}\sqrt{4\pi G},\quad \tilde{\Phi}=\frac{\Phi}{v_0^2}.
\label{eq:dim}
\end{equation}
Here, $v_0$ is a characteristic velocity. The SP equations can be rewritten as
\begin{align}
i \partial_{\tilde{t}}\tilde{\psi}(\mathbf{\tilde{x}},\tilde{t})&=-\frac{1}{2}\tilde{\nabla}^2\tilde{\psi}(\mathbf{\tilde{x}},\tilde{t})+\tilde{\Phi}(\mathbf{\tilde{x}},\tilde{t})\tilde{\psi}(\mathbf{\tilde{x}},\tilde{t}),
\label{eq:schrodinger_dim}
\\
\tilde{\nabla}^2\tilde{\Phi}(\mathbf{\tilde{x}},\tilde{t})&=|\tilde{\psi}(\mathbf{\tilde{x}},\tilde{t})|^2-\tilde{\overline{\rho}}.
\label{eq:poisson_dim}
\end{align}
For simplicity, we will omit the tilde symbols in the dimensionless quantities in the subsequent calculations. On timescales much shorter than the gravitational condensation timescale~\citep{Levkov:2018kau,Chen:2020cef}, the self-gravity of the homogeneous median can be ignored. The evolution of wavefunction in Fourier space can be solved analytically:
\begin{equation}
\psi(\mathbf{k},t)=\psi(\mathbf{k},0) e^{-\frac{1}{2} i \mathbf{k}^2 t}.
\label{eq:psi_k_t}
\end{equation}
The time correlation can then be written as
\begin{equation}
C_{\psi}(\mathbf{k},t)=\langle\psi^*(\mathbf{k},0)\psi(\mathbf{k},t)\rangle=P(\mathbf{k})e^{-\frac{1}{2} i \mathbf{k}^2 t},
\label{eq:C_psi_k_t}
\end{equation}
where $P(\mathbf{k})$ is the power spectrum of the wavefunction. Assuming the homogeneous median has a Maxwell–Boltzmann velocity distribution, $P(\mathbf{k})$ is given by
\begin{equation}
P(k)=\frac{\overline{\rho}}{(2\pi \sigma_J)^{3/2}}\exp\left(-\frac{k^2}{2\sigma_J^2}\right),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma_J$ is the $1-D$ velocity dispersion and $k=|\mathbf{k}|$. For a Gaussian power spectrum, the spatial and time correlation function of $\psi$ can be computed analytically by doing an inverse Fourier transform of Eq.~\eqref{eq:C_psi_k_t}:
\begin{equation}
C_{\psi}(r, t)=\overline{\rho}\,e^{\frac{i\,\sigma_J^2\,r^2}{2(1+i\,\sigma_J^2\,t)}} \left(1+i\,\sigma_J^2\,t\right)^{-3/2},
\label{eq:C_psi_x_t}
\end{equation}
where $r=|\mathbf{x}|$.

Having the correlation function for $\psi$, the density correlation function can be computed as
\begin{align}
\langle \rho(0,0) \rho(\mathbf{x},t)\rangle = &\langle \psi^*(0,0) \psi(0,0) \psi^*(\mathbf{x},t) \psi(\mathbf{x},t) \rangle \nonumber \\
=&\langle \psi^*(0,0) \psi(0,0)\rangle \langle \psi^*(\mathbf{x},t) \psi(\mathbf{x},t)\rangle \nonumber\\
& + \langle \psi^*(0,0) \psi^*(\mathbf{x},t)\rangle \langle \psi(0,0) \psi(\mathbf{x},t)\rangle \nonumber\\
& + \langle \psi^*(0,0) \psi(\mathbf{x},t)\rangle \langle \psi(0,0) \psi^*(\mathbf{x},t)\rangle \nonumber \\
=&\overline{\rho}^2 + \langle \psi^*(0,0) \psi(\mathbf{x},t)\rangle \langle \psi(0,0) \psi^*(\mathbf{x},t)\rangle \nonumber \\
=&\overline{\rho}^2 + |C_{\psi}(\mathbf{x},t)|^2.
\end{align}
Here we have used Wick's Theorem and the property $\langle \psi(0,0) \psi(\mathbf{x},t)\rangle=0$. Defining $C_{\rho}(r,t)=\langle \rho(0,0) \rho(\mathbf{x},t)\rangle-\overline{\rho}^2$ and plugging in Eq.~\eqref{eq:C_psi_x_t}, we have
\begin{equation}
C_{\rho}(r,t) = \overline{\rho}^2\,e^{-\frac{\sigma_J^2\,r^2}{1+\sigma_J^4\,t^2}} \left(1+\sigma_J^4\,t^2\right)^{-3/2}.
\label{eq:C_rho_x_t}
\end{equation}
Its Fourier transform with respect to the spatial coordinates is
\begin{equation}
C_{\rho}(k,t)=\frac{\overline{\rho}^2}{8\pi^{3/2}\sigma_J^3}\exp{\left(-\frac{k^2\left(1+\sigma_J^4\,t^2\right)}{4\sigma_J^2}\right)}.
\label{eq:C_rho_k_t}
\end{equation}

From the Poisson equation ~\eqref{eq:poisson_dim}, it is easy to find that
\begin{equation}
C_{\Phi}(k,t)\equiv\langle\Phi(\mathbf{k},0)\Phi(\mathbf{k},t)\rangle=\frac{C_{\rho}(k,t)}{k^4}.
\label{eq:C_Phi_k_t}
\end{equation}
Here $\Phi(k,t)$ is the Fourier transform of $\Phi(\mathbf{x},t)$. For acceleration $\mathbf{a}=-\nabla\Phi$, we have
\begin{equation}
C_{\mathbf{a}}(k,t)\equiv\langle \mathbf{a}(\mathbf{k},0) \mathbf{a}(\mathbf{k},t)\rangle=k^2 C_{\Phi}(k,t)=\frac{C_{\rho}(k,t)}{k^2}.
\label{eq:C_a_k_t}
\end{equation}
Performing an inverse Fourier transform, we obtain the correlation function for the acceleration field
\begin{equation}
C_{\mathbf{a}}(r,t)=\frac{\sqrt{\pi}\,\overline{\rho}^2}{4 \sigma_J^3\,r} \mathrm{erf}{\left(\frac{\sigma_J r}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_J^4\,t^2}}\right)}.
\label{eq:C_a_x_t_g}
\end{equation}
Plugging Eq.~\eqref{eq:C_a_x_t_g} into Eq.~\eqref{eq:sigma_pm}, we can get the velocity dispersion of the point-mass particle induced by the granule structure in the homogeneous medium. If the velocity of the test particle $\mathbf{v}=0$, the integral~\eqref{eq:sigma_pm} can be computed analytically:
\begin{align}
\sigma^2&=2\int_0^t(t-t')C_{\mathbf{a}}
(0,t')dt'\nonumber \\
&=\frac{\overline{\rho}^2}{\sigma_J^6}\left[1-\sqrt{1+\sigma_J^4\,t^2}+\sigma_J^2\,t \, \mathrm{arcsinh}\left(\sigma_J^2\,t\right)\right],
\label{eq:sigma_pm_v_0}
\end{align}
where $\mathrm{arcsinh}$ is the inverse of the hyperbolic sine function.

\subsection{Velocity Dispersion Anisotropy}
\label{sec:appendix_factorA}
In Appendix \ref{subsec:acc_point_mass} we derive the formula for the velocity dispersion of a point-mass particle induced by the granule structure. In reality, the induced velocity dispersion is not isotropic, which can be seen as follows. Assuming the particle moves in the $x$ direction, the velocity dispersions along different axes can be computed as
\begin{align}
\sigma_x^2&=2\int_0^t(t-t')C_{a_{\parallel}}(x=v\,t',y=0,z=0,t')dt',
\label{eq:sigma_pm_x}\\
\sigma_y^2&=\sigma_z^2=\int_0^t(t-t')C_{a_{\perp}}(x=v\,t',y=0,z=0,t')dt',
\label{eq:sigma_pm_y_z}
\end{align}
where $a_{\parallel}$ ($a_{\perp}$) is the acceleration parallel (perpendicular) to the moving direction of the particle. Similar to Eq.~\eqref{eq:C_a_k_t}, we have
\begin{align}
C_{a_{\parallel}}(k,t)&=\frac{k_x^2}{k^4}C_{\rho}(k,t),
\label{eq:C_a_par_k_t}\\
C_{a_{\perp}}(k,t)&=\frac{k_y^2+k_z^2}{k^4}C_{\rho}(k,t).
\label{eq:C_a_per_k_t}
\end{align}
We are only concerned with the spatial correlation along the moving direction ($x$ axis):
\begin{align}
 &C_{a_{\parallel}}(x=v\,t,y=0,z=0,t) \nonumber\\
=&\iiint C_{a_{\parallel}}(k,t) e^{ik_xx}dk_xd k_y dk_z \nonumber\\
=&\frac{\overline{\rho}^2\sqrt{1+\sigma_J^4\,t^2}}{4\sigma_J^4\,x^2}\left[-e^{-\frac{\sigma_J^2\,x^2}{1+\sigma_J^4\,t^2}}+\sqrt{\pi\left(1+\sigma_J^4\,t^2\right)}\,\frac{\mathrm{erf}\left(\frac{\sigma_J\,x}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_J^4\,t^2}}\right)}{2\sigma_J\,x}\right],
\label{eq:C_a_par_x_t}\\
 &C_{a_{\perp}}(x=v\,t,y=0,z=0,t) \nonumber\\
=&\iiint C_{a_{\perp}}(k,t) e^{ik_xx}dk_xd k_y dk_z \nonumber\\
=&\frac{\overline{\rho}^2\sqrt{1+\sigma_J^4\,t^2}}{4\sigma_J^4\,x^2} \nonumber\\
&\left[e^{-\frac{\sigma_J^2\,x^2}{1+\sigma_J^4\,t^2}}+\sqrt{\pi\left(1+\sigma_J^4\,t^2\right)}\left(-1+2\frac{\sigma_J^2\,x^2}{1+\sigma_J^4\,t^2}\right)\frac{\mathrm{erf}\left(\frac{\sigma_J\,x}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_J^4\,t^2}}\right)}{2\sigma_J\,x}\right].
\label{eq:C_a_per_x_t}
\end{align}
Plugging Eqs.~\eqref{eq:C_a_par_x_t} and \eqref{eq:C_a_per_x_t} into Eqs.~\eqref{eq:sigma_pm_x} and \eqref{eq:sigma_pm_y_z}, we can see that $\sigma_x^2\neq\sigma_y^2=\sigma_z^2$.

Fig.~\ref{fig:sigma_t} shows the total induced velocity dispersion $\sigma=\sqrt{\sigma_x^2+\sigma_y^2+\sigma_z^2}$ computed from Eqs.~\eqref{eq:sigma_pm} and \eqref{eq:C_a_x_t_g} for different initial velocities. Note that for simplicity, we have fixed the particle's trajectory, i.e., the particle always moves along the $x$ axis with its initial velocity. In reality, the trajectory of the particle will deviate from the initial direction when it is kicked by the granules. As can be seen, particles with higher initial velocities experience a slower growth in velocity dispersion compared to those initially at rest.

In addition to the reduction in the dispersion growth rate, the velocity dispersion exhibits a clear anisotropy. Fig.~\ref{fig:sigmaxy_t} presents the time evolution of the velocity dispersion components parallel ($\sigma_x$) and perpendicular ($\sigma_y$) to the direction of motion computed from Eqs.~\eqref{eq:sigma_pm_x}, \eqref{eq:sigma_pm_y_z}, \eqref{eq:C_a_par_x_t} and \eqref{eq:C_a_per_x_t}. For particles with non-zero initial velocities, the parallel component remains consistently smaller than the perpendicular one throughout the evolution.

