    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/sigma_t_v0.pdf}
    \caption{Time evolution of the velocity dispersion for particles with different initial velocities, shown in units of $\sigma_0\equiv\overline{\rho}/\sigma_J^3$. The black curve shows the reference case with zero initial velocity ($V_\mathrm{ini} = 0$). As the initial velocity increases, the growth of the velocity dispersion becomes progressively slower.}
    \label{fig:sigma_t}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/sigmaxy_t_v0.pdf}
    \caption{Time evolution of the velocity dispersion for particles with nonzero initial velocities, shown in units of $\sigma_0\equiv\overline{\rho}/\sigma_J^3$. The blue and orange curves show the components parallel, $\sigma_x$, and perpendicular, $\sigma_y$, to the direction of motion, respectively. The black curve shows the reference case with zero initial velocity ($V_\mathrm{ini} = 0$), scaled by $1/\sqrt{3}$. The parallel component remains systematically smaller than the perpendicular one throughout the evolution, reflecting that the velocity dispersion becomes increasingly anisotropic with higher initial velocities.}
    \label{fig:sigmaxy_t}
\end{figure}

We interpret this anisotropic behaviour in the velocity dispersion by introducing a velocity-dependent time rescaling factor $A$, which modifies the effective amplitude of the stochastic acceleration. Let $\sigma(t)$ denote the velocity dispersion for particles with zero initial velocity. For particles with nonzero initial velocity $v_\mathrm{norm}$, the time evolution of the velocity dispersion can be approximated as
\begin{equation}
    \sigma(t;\,v_\mathrm{norm}) \sim \frac{\sigma(At)}{A}.
\end{equation}
Assuming $\sigma(t)\propto \sqrt{t}$, this leads to
\begin{equation}
    \frac{\sigma(At)}{A} \propto \frac{\sigma(t)}{\sqrt{A}},
\end{equation}
which implies that any rescaling of the time variable $t$ is equivalent to a modification of the amplitude of the acceleration. Based on this relation, we model the time rescaling factors for the total and perpendicular components of the velocity dispersion as 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:Atotal, Aperp}
    A_\mathrm{total} = \sqrt{1 + \left( \frac{v_{\mathrm{norm}}}{\sigma_J} \right)^2},\, A_{\perp} = \sqrt{1 + \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{v_{\mathrm{norm}}}{\sigma_J} \right)^2}.
\end{equation}
The parallel component is then computed from
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:Aparallel}
    A_{\parallel} = \left( \frac{3}{A_{\mathrm{total}}} - \frac{2}{A_{\perp}} \right)^{-1}.
\end{equation}

These proposed expressions for the rescaling factors are validated by comparison with results extracted from semi-analytic calculations across a range of initial velocities. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:A}, the analytic forms for $A_\mathrm{total}$, $A_\perp$, and $A_\parallel$ provide excellent agreement with the extracted results.

