    \label{fig:A}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Finite-size objects}

We consider the granule-induced acceleration field $\mathbf{a}(\mathbf{x})$ as a statistically homogeneous Gaussian random field. The effective acceleration experienced by a subhalo, denoted $\mathbf{a}_\mathrm{sub}$, is computed by convolving this field $\mathbf{a}(\mathbf{x})$ with the subhalo's density profile. Specifically, we evaluate the spatial average:
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{a}_{\mathrm{sub}}(\mathbf{x}) = \frac{1}{M_{\mathrm{sub}}} \int \rho_{\mathrm{sub}}(|\mathbf{x}' - \mathbf{x}|)\, \mathbf{a}(\mathbf{x}')\, d^3\mathbf{x}',
\end{equation}
where $\rho_\mathrm{sub}$ is the spherically symmetric density profile of the subhalo and $M_\mathrm{sub}$ is its total mass. Under the assumption of Gaussian statistics, the variance of $\mathbf{a}_\mathrm{sub}$ can be calculated in Fourier space as
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:sigma_asub}
    \sigma^2_{\mathbf{a}_{\mathrm{sub}}} = \int P_{\mathbf{a}_{\mathrm{sub}}}(k)\, d^3\mathbf{k} = \frac{1}{M_{\mathrm{sub}}^2} \int P_{\rho_{\mathrm{sub}}}(k)\, P_{\mathbf{a}}(k)\, d^3\mathbf{k},
\end{equation}
where $P_{\rho_{\mathrm{sub}}}(k)$ and $P_{\mathbf{a}}(k)$ are the power spectra of the subhalo's density profile and that of the acceleration field, respectively. The variance is determined by the product of the subhalo’s density power spectrum and the power spectrum of the acceleration field.

We test this approach using three representative cases, shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:sigma_a_granule}. The open circles represent measurements extracted from the simulation output, while the solid curves correspond to theoretical predictions obtained from the convolution of the density and acceleration power spectra.

We consider constant-density subhalos of varying radii, evaluated in an acceleration field with a white-noise power spectrum--i.e., spatially uncorrelated in structure, shown as the blue points in Fig.~\ref{fig:sigma_a_granule}. In this case, the acceleration variance follows the expected Poisson scaling, $\sigma_a \propto N^{-1/2} \propto R^{-3/2}$, consistent with the fact that smaller volumes average over fewer independent realizations of the field. To isolate the effect of field correlations, we compute the acceleration variance using the same subhalo density profile but with an acceleration field generated from FDM granules, shown as the orange points. Unlike the white-noise case, this field is spatially correlated on the de Broglie wavelength scale, which leads to a larger acceleration variance compared to the uncorrelated case. The green points represent subhalos with an NFW density profile, evaluated in the same acceleration field generated from FDM granules. The slightly lower variance observed for NFW subhalos, particularly at small radii, is due to their centrally concentrated density profiles, which suppress low-$k$ modes in the density power spectrum. As a result, the NFW subhalos are less sensitive to the large-scale acceleration fluctuations, leading to a reduced acceleration variance compared to constant-density subhalos.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/a_finite_size.pdf}
    \caption{Acceleration variance $\sigma_a$ as a function of subhalo size $R$, for different combinations of subhalo density profiles and acceleration field structures. Open circles show simulation results; solid curves are computed from the convolution of power spectra $P_{\rho_{\mathrm{sub}}}(k)$ and $P_{\mathbf{a}}(k)$, where $P_{\rho_{\mathrm{sub}}}(k)$ is the power spectra of the subhalo density profile and $P_{\mathbf{a}}(k)$ is that of the granule acceleration field. The blue points correspond to constant-density subhalos in uncorrelated (white-noise) acceleration fields, showing Poisson scaling $\sigma_a\propto R^{-1.5}$. The orange points use the same subhalo profile but a granule-generated acceleration field, which is spatially correlated on the de Broglie wavelength scale. The green points represent NFW subhalos in the same correlated field. For NFW subhalos with sizes larger than $d_{\mathrm{eff}}$, the acceleration variance decreases following a power law (black dashed curve).}
    \label{fig:sigma_a_granule}
\end{figure}

The theoretical predictions for the acceleration variance, $\sigma_{a_x}$, are in good agreement with the values measured from simulations across all three cases. For an NFW subhalo, $\sigma_{a_x}$ decreases with increasing $r_{\mathrm{half}}$, following a power-law $\propto (r_{\mathrm{half}}/d_{\mathrm{eff}})^{-0.41}$. Note that, to facilitate comparison with simulation results, we impose lower and upper limits on the wavenumber when calculating the integral in Eq.~\eqref{eq:sigma_asub}. Specifically, we set $k_{\mathrm{min}}=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{3}L}$ and $k_{\mathrm{max}}=\frac{\sqrt{3}\pi}{\Delta x}$ corresponding to maximum and minimum correlation lengths in a finite box, where $L$ is the size of simulation box and $\Delta x$ is the spatial resolution. Physically, if the subhalo is orbiting in a host halo of finite size, $k_{\mathrm{min}}$ should be determined by the size of the host halo. This also explains why the coefficient $\alpha$ obtained by fitting to the homogenous background simulations (see Sec.~\ref{sec:finite_size_calibration}) differs slightly from the value derived from the simulations of a realistic halo (see Sec.~\ref{sec:point_mass}).

The induced velocity dispersion not only depends on the variance of the acceleration field, but also depends on its spatial and time correlation, as shown in Appendix~\ref{subsec:acc_point_mass}. For finite-size objects, Eq.~\eqref{eq:C_a_k_t} is modified as
\begin{equation}
C_{\mathbf{a}}(k,t)^{\mathrm{sub}}=\frac{P_{\rho_{\mathrm{sub}}}(k)}{M_{\mathrm{sub}^2}}\frac{C_{\rho}(k,t)}{k^2}.
\label{eq:C_a_k_t_finite_size}
\end{equation}
Unlike the point-mass case, the inverse Fourier transform of $C_{\mathbf{a}}(k,t)^{\mathrm{sub}}$ needs to be computed numerically. With the above equation, we can compute the induced velocity dispersion of an object with an arbitrary density profile.

Fig.~\ref{fig:sigma_ratio_finite_size} shows the velocity dispersion of a finite-size object relative to the point-mass case at $t=50\,t_{\rm granule}$. The analytic predictions (blue solid curve), computed from Eqs.~\eqref{eq:C_a_k_t_finite_size} and \eqref{eq:sigma_pm}, agree well with the direct numerical simulations assuming NFW profiles for the subhalos (circles). When the half-radius is larger than $d_{\rm eff}$, the velocity dispersion decreases approximately as $(r_{\rm half}/d_{\rm eff})^{-0.229}$, consistent with the size-dependence of the coefficient $\alpha$, see Fig.~\ref{fig:alpha_finite}.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/sigma_ratio_finite_size.pdf}
    \caption{Velocity dispersion of NFW subhalos relative to the point-mass particle case at $t=50\,t_{\rm granule}$. The circles show the numerical simulation results, while the blue solid curve is computed from Eqs.~\eqref{eq:C_a_k_t_finite_size} and \eqref{eq:sigma_pm}. The vertical dashed line marks the spatial resolution limit of the simulation. At $r_{\rm half}/d_{\rm eff} \gtrsim 1$, the ratio $\sigma/\sigma^P$ scales approximately as $(r_{\rm half}/d_{\rm eff})^{-0.229}$, consistent with the size-dependence of the coefficient $\alpha$ obtained in Sec.~\ref{sec:finite_size_calibration}.}
    \label{fig:sigma_ratio_finite_size}
\end{figure}

