\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Trophic Properties}

We analyse two aspects of the trophic structure in relation to symmetric orbits: the trophic span and the mean trophic level.

The trophic span of an orbit is defined as the difference between the maximum and minimum trophic level of the orbit's vertices. When no aggregation is used, vertices can only be symmetric if they have exactly the same trophic level. Fig.~\ref{fig: OrbitTrophic} (left) shows that in the approximations $\Phi_1, \Phi_2, \Phi_4$ the trophic span remains below 0.5. In contrast, $\Phi_\infty$ which ignores edge weights, reveals that similarity in ecosystem functions can relate groups at more distant trophic levels.

