The analysis of the community structure from one giant connected component after applying the threshold ${\theta_{\rho} = 0.5}$ from the price correlation networks shows that the average modularity $\left\langle Q \right\rangle$ values for each strategy across $n=30$ realisations for each period are relatively low (Tab.\ref{tab:AvgQ_tables}), with values around 0.12. Modularity values range from $-1$ to 1, where values closer to 1 indicate a strong and well-defined community structure. Thus, the observed low modularity values suggest a weak modular or clustered structure. Among all the strategies, those without sliding windows, specifically the Baseline and P(ARIMA), resulted in slightly higher average modularity $\left\langle Q \right\rangle$. Conversely, while sliding window strategies, such as S and P(ARIMA)-S, demonstrate lower standard deviations $\sigma$, reflecting their higher consistency in cluster definitions across different periods, at the cost of reduced modularity. 


