\section{Conclusion}
In this article we have dealt with inflationary equation-of-state formalism in the light of recent ACT-DR6 data. We find that the Mukhanov parametrization of inflationary equation-of-state does provide an excellent match with recent ACT-DR6 data. We also find that  one of the model parameter, $\beta$, is highly sensitive to the  value of tensor-to-scalar ratio along with the number of e-foldings.  While the range for $\alpha$ is mainly dependent on the constraint for scalar spectral index and $N$. Moreover, the range of the model parameter $\alpha$ is amplified when we also utilize the prediction for non-detection of primordial gravity waves from the futuristic CMB experiments  LikeBIRD and/or CMB-S4. But the situation is opposite for $\beta$ as the observationally viable range is reduced considerably.

The combination of  ACT-DR6, Planck-2018 and DESI-Y1 data is found to provide more accurate result where error-bars on spectral index is much smaller compared to the result of ACT DR-6 data alone.  In this case we were able to constrain the model parameters more precisely. The parameter space for equation-of-state formalism is significantly reduced when non-detection of primordial gravity waves from the futuristic CMB experiments  LikeBIRD and/or CMB-S4 is also taken into consideration. 

In Table \ref{table_summary} we have tabulated the upper and lower bounds of the model parameter $\alpha$ for two different values of e-foldings. Here in this table we have compared combined result of ACT-DR6, Planck-2018 and DESI-Y1 data on top predictions from LiteBIRD and CMB-S4 with stand alone ACT-DR6. We find that combined analysis ACT-DR6, Planck-2018 and DESI-Y1 data has narrowed the range for $\alpha$ considerable in compare to the ACT-DR6 data alone. However, non-detection of primordial gravity waves by LikeBIRD and/or CMB-S4 may slightly increase the observational window for $\alpha$. 
