\begin{abstract}
\begin{spacing}{1.2}
The Diversification Quotient (DQ), introduced by \cite{HLW25}, is a recently proposed measure of portfolio diversification that quantifies the reduction in a portfolio's risk-level parameter attributable to diversification. Grounded in a rigorous theoretical framework,  DQ  effectively captures heavy tails, common shocks, and enhances efficiency in portfolio optimization. This paper further explores the convergence properties and asymptotic normality of empirical DQ estimators based on Value at Risk (VaR) and Expected Shortfall (ES), with explicit calculation of the asymptotic variance. In contrast to the diversification ratio (DR) proposed by \cite{T07}, which may exhibit diverging asymptotic variance due to its lack of location invariance, the DQ estimators demonstrate greater robustness under various distributional settings. We further evaluate their performance under elliptical distributions and conduct a simulation study to examine their finite-sample behavior. The results offer a solid statistical foundation for the application of DQ in financial risk management and decision-making.
\medskip
\noindent

\textsc{Keywords:} diversification quotient, convergence, asymptotic normality, asymptotic variance, Expected Shortfall, Value at Risk
\end{spacing}
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
  Diversification is a fundamental principle in portfolio management, designed to reduce risk by combining assets with different risk profiles. A variety of indices have been proposed to quantify diversification. Traditional measures, such as the Diversification Ratio (e.g., \cite{T07}, \cite{BDI08}  and 
   \cite{EWW15}) and Diversification Benefit (DB,  e.g., \cite{EFK09} and \cite{MFE15}), primarily focus on changes in capital requirements resulting from asset pooling.  
 In this context, the Diversification Quotient (DQ), introduced by \cite{HLW25}, has emerged as a promising alternative. The DQ quantifies the improvement in a portfolio’s risk-level parameter due to diversification and is grounded in six axioms: non-negativity, location invariance, scale
invariance, rationality, normalization, and continuity.  These axioms provide a solid theoretical foundation, distinguishing DQ from other measures and ensuring its robustness in various financial applications.



In financial risk management, Value at Risk (VaR) and Expected Shortfall (ES) are widely adopted, especially in regulatory frameworks like Basel III/IV and Solvency II. The DQs based on VaR and ES have proven advantageous in capturing heavy tails, common shocks, and their efficiency in portfolio optimization, as demonstrated by \cite{HLW23, HLW25}.  In addition to VaR and ES, expectiles have emerged as an important class of risk measures. Given their significance, \cite{HLWW24} extends the DQ framework by incorporating expectiles, thereby broadening the toolkit available for risk management beyond VaR and ES.  These studies also investigate additional properties of DQs  within popular risk management models like  elliptical and multivariate regularly varying
distribution models.



  This paper extends existing theoretical work by investigating the convergence properties and asymptotic normality of the empirical estimators for the DQ.  In fact, the estimation of VaR and ES has been widely studied in financial risk management. Early parametric methods, such as Gaussian and t-distributions, as well as more advanced models like ARCH and GARCH, are commonly employed to model the return distributions; see e.g., \cite{EKM97}, \cite{FM02} and \cite{MFE15}. While these parametric models are efficient when the underlying assumptions are correct, they are highly sensitive to model misspecification. In contrast, nonparametric methods have gained attention for their ability to avoid distributional assumptions and capture fat tails and asymmetry in financial data automatically.

 Nonparametric methods typically require larger sample sizes but offer a robust alternative by mitigating the risk of model misspecification.  Various estimator techniques have been developed to improve accuracy and reduce model risk, making nonparametric approaches an attractive choice in many financial applications. For instance, \cite{S04} proposed a nonparametric kernel estimator and applied it to sensitivity analysis in the context of portfolio allocation for ES. \cite{CT05} explored nonparametric inference of VaR for dependent financial returns. Importance sampling techniques for parametric portfolio credit risk models were detailed by \cite{G05} and \cite{GL05} for both VaR and ES.  \cite{C08} considered two nonparametric ES estimators for dependent financial losses: one based on the sample average of excessive losses larger than VaR, and another as a kernel-smoothed version of the first estimator. Kernel estimation and importance sampling techniques were combined by \cite{T09} for a portfolio credit risk model. \cite{H09} employed nonparametric estimations via infinitesimal perturbation analysis.  For expectile-based risk measures, \cite{KZ17} examined several properties of  consistency and asymptotic normality,  and \cite{MBL21}   constructed a
kernel-type estimator of the expectile regression function.    More recently, nonparametric estimation has been applied to risk measures induced by VaR and ES,  as shown in \cite{APWY19}, \cite{BFWW22}, and \cite{LW23}. 

 Since the DQ, when based on law-invariant risk measures, inherits the property of law-invariance, it can be estimated using the empirical distribution of portfolio losses.  We first establish the consistency of the DQ estimator for a class of risk measures that satisfy the continuity condition, which applies to a broad range of risk measures (Theorem \ref{thm:1}). We derive the asymptotic normality and explicitly compute the asymptotic variances of empirical DQ estimators based on VaR and ES under the i.i.d. setting (Theorems \ref{thm:2} and \ref{thm:3}).  To account for temporal dependence commonly observed in financial data, we further extend the results to 
$\alpha$-mixing sequences. Under this more general framework, the estimators remain consistent and asymptotically normal (Theorems \ref{thm:4} and \ref{thm:5}), ensuring robustness in practical applications. %Our results enable decision makers to apply standard statistical methods, such as hypothesis testing and confidence intervals, to assess the precision and reliability of DQ estimates.  
Building on the explicit formulas for DQ based on VaR and ES, we further investigate the performance of their empirical estimators under the assumption that the portfolio follows an elliptical distribution. In particular, we analyze how key factors—such as the confidence level, correlation structure, portfolio dimension, and degrees of freedom—affect the asymptotic variance of the DQ estimators. We also establish the asymptotic normality and derive the asymptotic variance of the DR based on VaR and ES (Proposition \ref{prop:5}). To the best of our knowledge, these theoretical results for DR have not been previously documented in the literature, and thus represent a novel contribution. However, DR lacks location invariance, which can lead to instability: under certain location shifts, the asymptotic variance of DR may diverge. In contrast, DQ is location-invariant, providing greater robustness in such settings.




The remainder of the paper is structured as follows. Section \ref{sec:2} reviews the definition of the DQ and introduces its empirical estimator based on observed data. Section \ref{sec:3} establishes the consistency of the empirical DQ estimators derived from a general class of risk measures. In Section \ref{sec:4}, we examine the asymptotic normality of DQ, with a particular focus on the cases where the underlying risk measures are VaR and ES. Section \ref{sec:5} extends these results to settings with dependent data, while Section \ref{sec:6} presents the corresponding asymptotic theory for the DR. Section \ref{sec:7} provides numerical illustrations that highlight the behavior of DQ under elliptical distributional assumptions. Section \ref{sec:8} concludes. Given the growing prominence of expectiles as a class of risk measures, Appendix \ref{App:A} is devoted to analyzing the asymptotic behavior of DQ based on expectiles. All technical proofs are collected in Appendix \ref{App:B}.



\section{Definitions and preliminary conclusions}
\label{sec:2}
 Throughout this paper, we work on an atomless probability space $(\Omega,\;\mathcal{F},\;\mathbb{P})$. The assumption for atomless space is widely used in statistics and risk management; for further details, see \cite{D02} and Section A.3 of \cite{FS16}. 
 Let $\X$ be a convex cone of random variables on $(\Omega,\;\mathcal{F},\;\mathbb{P})$ where each $X \in \X$ represents the loss of a financial product. Let $L^1$ be  the set of all integrable variables on $(\Omega,\;\mathcal{F},\;\mathbb{P})$. Write $X\sim F$  if the random variable $X$ has the distribution function $F$  under $\mathbb{P}$, and  $X \laweq  Y$ if two random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the same distribution. 
	We always write $\mathbf X=(X_1,\dots,X_n)$ as a random vector and $S=\sum_{i=1}^n X_i$ as the sum of $\mathbf X$.  
	Further, denote by $[n]=\{1,\dots,n\}$.  Terms such as increasing or decreasing functions are in the non-strict sense. 
 A risk measure $\phi$ is a mapping from $\X$ to $\R$.  We will assume law-invariance for risk measures throughout the work.
 \begin{enumerate}
     \item[{[LI]}] Law-invariance: $\rho(X)=\rho(Y)$ for any $X, Y \in \X$ such that $X\laweq Y$. 
 \end{enumerate}
Law-invariance ensures that statistical measures depend only on the distribution of random variables, which allows us to estimate the value of risk measures based on the distributions of risks.
  % We assume that all risk measures $\phi$ in this work are law-invariant; that is,  $\rho(X)=\rho(Y)$ for any $X, Y \in \X$ such that $X\laweq Y$. 
% Let $\mathcal M$ represent the set of cumulative distribution functions (cdfs) for all random variables in $\X$. Since the value of a law-invariant risk measure is determined by the distributions of random variables, we also define risk measure $\phi$ on $\mathcal M$,  which makes no difference for the understanding. Thus,  we also write $\rho(F)$ to denote $\rho(X)$ for $X \sim F$.
We collect some other properties of risk measures below.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[{[M]}] Monotonicity:  $\phi(X)\leq\phi(Y)$ for all $X, Y \in \X$ with  $X\leq Y$.
\item[{[TI]}] Translation invariance: $\phi(X+m)=\phi(X)+m$ for all $X \in \X$ and   $m\in\mathbb{R}$. 
\item[{[CV]}] Convexity: 
$\phi\left(\gamma X+(1-\gamma)Y\right)\leq\gamma \phi(X)+(1-\gamma )\phi(Y)$ for all  $X, Y \in \X$ and  $\gamma\in [0,1]$.
\end{enumerate} 
A   risk measure satisfies [M], [TI] and [CV] is said to be \emph{convex}. In risk management, many commonly used risk measures belong to a parametric family. For example,  two  most popular classes of risk measures in banking and insurance, Value-at-Risk (VaR) and Expected Shortfall (ES), are indexed by probability level.   The definitions of the two risk measures are provided below.
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item The Value at Risk (VaR) at level $\alpha \in [0,1)$ is defined by
$$
\VaR_\alpha(X)=\inf\{x\in \R: \p(X\le x) \ge 1-\alpha\}~~~X\in L^0.
$$
\item The Expected Shortfall (ES, also called CVaR, TVaR or AVaR) at level $\alpha \in (0,1)$ is defined by
$$
	\ES_{\alpha}(X) = \frac 1 \alpha \int_{0}^\alpha \VaR_\beta(X) \d \beta,~~~X\in L^1,
	$$
    and $\ES_0(X)=\esssup(X):=\VaR_0(X)$.
\end{enumerate}
We use the ``small-$\alpha$" definition here. 


\cite{HLW25} define the diversification quotient  (DQ)  based on a class of parameteric risk measures as following. 

%For $\alpha\in (0,\overline  \alpha)$ with $\overline \alpha\in(0,\infty]$, and  a class of risk measures $\rho=\{\rho_{\beta}\}_{\beta\in (0,\alpha)}$ which satisfied $\rho_{\beta}$ is decreasing about $\beta$,   the  \emph{diversification quotient} (DQ)  is defined as follows.

\begin{definition}[Diversification Quotient]\label{def:DQ}
Let $  \rho =(\rho_{\alpha})_{\alpha \in  I}$ be a class of risk measures indexed by $\alpha\in I=(0,\overline \alpha) $ with $\overline \alpha\in(0,\infty]$ such that $\rho_\alpha$ is decreasing in $\alpha$. 
  For $\mathbf X \in \X^n$, the \emph{diversification quotient}  based on the class $ \rho$  at level $\alpha\in I$ is defined  by
 \begin{equation}\label{eq:DQ}
 {\rm DQ}^{\rho}_\alpha(\mathbf X)=\frac{\alpha^*}{\alpha}, \mbox{~~
where }
 \alpha^*= \inf\left\{\beta \in I :  \rho_{\beta} \left(\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\right) \le \sum_{i=1}^n \rho_{\alpha}(X_i) \right\}, 
 \end{equation} with the convention $\inf(\varnothing)=\overline \alpha$.
\end{definition}
It is clear that $\VaR_\alpha$ and $\ES_\alpha$  are decreasing with $\alpha$. When employing these risk measures, we typically prefer a level $\alpha$ close to zero, such as 0.01 or 0.025 in \cite{BASEL19}. By taking $\rho$ as  $(\VaR_\alpha)_{\alpha \in (0,1)}$ or $(\ES_\alpha)_{\alpha \in (0,1)}$,  we have the two diversification indices $\DQVaR$ and $\DQES$ both of which have nice properties in terms of risk management; see \cite{HLW23, HLW25}  for more discussion.  

 In the next result, we show that DQ based on a class of law-invariant risk measures is also law-invariant.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:1}
Let $\rho$ be a class of law-invariant risk measures. Then, we have $\DQrho(\mathbf X)=\DQrho(\mathbf Y)$ for any $\mathbf X, \mathbf Y \in \X^n$ such that $\mathbf X \laweq\mathbf Y$.
\end{proposition}


Naturally, for DQ based on law-invariant risk measure class, we can expect DQ is also law-invariant. For a class of law-invariant risk measures $\rho$, the value of DQ is determined by the joint distribution of the portfolio risk $\bX$. In this way, we can estimate the value of a law-invariant diversification index by the empirical distribution of the portfolio loss $\bX=(X_1, \dots, X_n)$.
Below, we simply write $\mathrm{DQ}_\alpha^\rho(F)=\mathrm{DQ}^\rho_\alpha(\bX)$ for $\bX \sim F$.




