\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:8}
This paper investigates the asymptotic behavior of empirical DQ estimators when the underlying risk measure is law-invariant, with particular emphasis on VaR, ES, and expectiles. We establish both consistency and asymptotic normality under i.i.d. and $\alpha$-mixing settings, thereby extending the theoretical foundations of DQ in realistic financial environments. In addition, we provide a new asymptotic analysis of the DR and compare its properties with those of DQ under elliptical distributions. The results reveal that although DR is widely used, its lack of location invariance may lead to instability under location shifts—a limitation not shared by DQ.

Looking ahead, several promising directions for future research remain. One important direction is the development of bootstrap and other finite-sample inference methods for DQ, grounded in real data analysis, which would enhance its practical applicability. Another is the integration of DQ into constrained portfolio optimization problems. These extensions would deepen the theoretical understanding of diversification measures and strengthen their relevance to modern risk management.

